window.AppNhoPhase17EN = {"categories":{"Nền tảng":"Foundations","Denim":"Denim","Sportswear":"Sportswear","Outerwear":"Outerwear","Intimates":"Intimates","Childrenwear":"Childrenwear","Workwear":"Workwear","Medical / hygiene":"Medical and Hygiene","Insulated garments":"Insulated Garments","Special use":"Specialized Use","Special materials":"Special Materials","Kỹ thuật tích hợp":"Integrated Engineering","Kiểm soát chất lượng":"Quality Control","Chuỗi cung ứng":"Supply Chain","Thực hành chuyên sâu":"Advanced Practice"},"ui":{"breadcrumb":"← Garment Academy Library","academy_small":"GARMENT KNOWLEDGE LIBRARY","ga_nav_1":"Materials and Textiles","ga_nav_2":"Design and Products","ga_nav_3":"Manufacturing","ga_nav_4":"Quality","ga_nav_5":"Management and Commercial","ga_nav_6":"Dictionary","page_eyebrow":"GARMENT ACADEMY · KNOWLEDGE LIBRARY","page_title":"Specialized Garment Manufacturing Technology","page_intro":"Denim, sportswear, swimwear, intimate apparel, children’s wear, workwear, PPE, outerwear, medical garments, cleanroom garments, and multi-layer technical products.","scope_note":"Scope: This page addresses use requirements, risks, materials, and integrated engineering for specialized product groups. The general development process is covered in ","scope_link1":"Fashion Design and Product Development","scope_mid":"; detailed garment construction and assembly techniques are covered in ","scope_link2":"Garment Construction","scope_mid2":". Advanced material knowledge and testing are linked to ","scope_link3":"Advanced Materials","scope_and":" and ","scope_link4":"Textile Testing Laboratory","scope_end":".","flow_title":"Specialized Product Development Sequence","flow_use":"Use case","flow_hazard":"hazard / activity","flow_design":"Design","flow_material":"material / pattern","flow_validate":"Validation","flow_lab":"lab / wear trial","flow_pilot":"Pilot","flow_machine":"machine / process","flow_release":"Bulk release","flow_trace":"traceability / control","flow_footer":"Every claim must be linked to a hazard, test method, acceptance criterion, and approval record.","search_placeholder":"Search product groups, materials, technologies, or risks…","category_all":"All content groups","stats":"Filtered by content group","nav_label":"Topic contents","warning_title":"Warning about protective products","warning_text":"This material does not certify PPE or replace risk assessment, legal standards, accredited testing, or manufacturer instructions. Do not use a product outside its verified protection scope.","practice_heading":"Technical Analysis and Application","no_results_title":"No matching lesson found","no_results_text":"Try another keyword or select all content groups.","scope_note_html":"<strong>Scope:</strong> This page addresses use requirements, risks, materials, and integrated engineering for specialized product groups. The general development process is covered in <a href=\"Phase16_Fashion_Design_Product_Development.html\">Fashion Design and Product Development</a>; detailed garment construction and assembly techniques are covered in <a href=\"Phase6_Garment_Construction.html\">Garment Construction</a>. Advanced material knowledge and testing are linked to <a href=\"Phase13_Advanced_Materials.html\">Advanced Materials</a> and <a href=\"Phase15_Textile_Testing_Laboratory.html\">Textile Testing Laboratory</a>.","flow_aria":"Specialized garment product development process","document_title":"Garment Academy | Specialized Garment Manufacturing Technology","meta_description":"Technical learning resources on denim, sportswear, swimwear, intimate apparel, workwear, PPE, outerwear, and specialized garment production."},"lessons":{"1":{"title":"Classification of Specialized Garment Products","cat":"Foundations","body":["Specialized garments are designed for defined conditions of use, including intense movement, weather exposure, contact with water, friction, chemicals, heat, dust, or hygiene requirements. Product construction must be derived from actual activities and risks, not merely from the commercial product category.","Distinguish ordinary fashion products from products carrying protective claims. For claims such as waterproof, flame-resistant, antistatic, or protective, identify the applicable standards, target market, and test scope before the design is finalized.","A development matrix should record the use case, wearer, environment, hazards, materials, construction, test methods, acceptance criteria, and limitations of use. A product may belong to more than one category, so overlapping requirements must be controlled.","**Design rationale and scope.** Classify specialized garments by task and conditions of use. Denim, sportswear, outerwear, intimate apparel, children’s wear, workwear, and PPE present different technical risks. A product may fall into several categories; for example, a sports jacket may need rain protection, moisture management, and abrasion resistance at the same time.","**Implementation process and control records.** For each style, record the wearer, activity, environment, duration, care cycles, hazards, claims, materials, construction, test methods, and target market. Distinguish ordinary functional apparel from PPE carrying a protective claim. Identify market-specific standards and regulations before confirming a claim, then create a traceability matrix linking each requirement to product construction and test evidence.","**Case analysis and verification.** Do not classify a product by its trade name alone. A “waterproof” jacket requires assessment of the fabric, seams, zippers, storm flaps, and complete garment construction; a “high-visibility” garment requires assessment of the fluorescent background material, retroreflective material, and overall configuration. Where several functions are combined, assess their interactions rather than approving each feature in isolation."],"deep":"Classify products by environment, task, and the consequences of product failure. A sports jacket, rain jacket, and protective garment may use similar knitted or woven fabrics but have different design criteria. Identify the wearer group, frequency of use, washing cycles, expected service life, and storage conditions. Correct classification helps determine the appropriate test methods and technical records.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Classify specialized garments by task and conditions of use. Denim, sportswear, outerwear, intimate apparel, children’s wear, workwear, and PPE present different technical risks. A product may fall into several categories; for example, a sports jacket may need rain protection, moisture management, and abrasion resistance at the same time.\n\n**Development and production controls.** For each style, record the wearer, activity, environment, duration, care cycles, hazards, claims, materials, construction, test methods, and target market. Distinguish ordinary functional apparel from PPE carrying a protective claim. Identify market-specific standards and regulations before confirming a claim, then create a traceability matrix linking each requirement to product construction and test evidence.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** Do not classify a product by its trade name alone. A “waterproof” jacket requires assessment of the fabric, seams, zippers, storm flaps, and complete garment construction; a “high-visibility” garment requires assessment of the fluorescent background material, retroreflective material, and overall configuration. Where several functions are combined, assess their interactions rather than approving each feature in isolation.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a use case–risk–construction–test matrix for a product in this group. Record the market, user, conditions of use, required documents, sample configuration, and evidence needed before approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Classification of Specialized Garment Products\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Classification of Specialized Garment Products</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Classification of Specialized Garment Products</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Requirements</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Construction</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Verification</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Approval</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"2":{"title":"Use-Case Analysis and Activity-Based Risk Assessment","cat":"Foundations","body":["Describe the activity in sequence, including posture, movement frequency, contact areas, loads, environment, and wearing time. For example, a motorcyclist exposed to wind and rain has different needs from a runner, even if both wear a lightweight jacket.","Use hazard analysis to identify risks such as entanglement, snagging, slipping, overheating, loss of visibility, water penetration, seam failure, or material degradation. Assess severity and likelihood before selecting a design solution.","Convert each risk into a verifiable requirement. If no suitable test method or supporting data is available, record the issue as an open risk and do not make claims that exceed the evidence.","**Design rationale and scope.** A use case describes a user performing a task in a defined context. Risk analysis identifies what could cause harm or prevent the product from functioning during that task. Consider probability, severity of consequences, detectability, and groups of users with different exposure levels.","**Implementation process and control records.** Break the activity into steps, postures, environmental conditions, and contact points. Record hazards involving entanglement, snagging, slipping, overheating, reduced visibility, restricted movement, chemicals, UV exposure, or material degradation. Link each hazard to a design characteristic, mitigation measure, test method, and limitation of use. Involve users or operating personnel to confirm that the scenario reflects actual conditions.","**Case analysis and verification.** An external hanging cord on a worker’s garment may catch in machinery. The solution may require removing or repositioning the cord, not merely increasing its strength. Where risk is high, do not use an internal test that lacks the required capability as the basis for a protective claim. Refer the requirement to the authorized safety or compliance function."],"deep":"A use case describes a sequence of real actions, while risk analysis identifies what could happen at each step. Prepare a table covering activity, body area, hazard source, exposure time, environmental conditions, and consequences. Then define the required product feature, verification method, and residual risk. Do not infer a level of protection from the product name or appearance alone.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** A use case describes a user performing a task in a defined context. Risk analysis identifies what could cause harm or prevent the product from functioning during that task. Consider probability, severity of consequences, detectability, and groups of users with different exposure levels.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Break the activity into steps, postures, environmental conditions, and contact points. Record hazards involving entanglement, snagging, slipping, overheating, reduced visibility, restricted movement, chemicals, UV exposure, or material degradation. Link each hazard to a design characteristic, mitigation measure, test method, and limitation of use. Involve users or operating personnel to confirm that the scenario reflects actual conditions.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** An external hanging cord on a worker’s garment may catch in machinery. The solution may require removing or repositioning the cord, not merely increasing its strength. Where risk is high, do not use an internal test that lacks the required capability as the basis for a protective claim. Refer the requirement to the authorized safety or compliance function.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a use case–risk–construction–test matrix for a product in this group. Record the market, user, conditions of use, required documents, sample configuration, and evidence needed before approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Use-Case Analysis and Activity-Based Risk Assessment\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Use-Case Analysis and Activity-Based Risk Assessment</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use-Case Analysis and Activity-Based Risk Assessment</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Requirements</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Construction</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Verification</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Approval</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"3":{"title":"Denim: Fabric Structure and Raw-Material Characteristics","cat":"Denim","body":["Traditional denim is generally a woven twill fabric, often using indigo-dyed warp yarns and lighter-coloured weft yarns. Weave, density, yarn count, twist, slub, and fibre composition determine the surface appearance, strength, and fading behaviour.","Stretch denim may contain elastane or core-spun stretch yarns. Comfort-stretch and rigid denim behave differently in wear, recovery, and dimensional stability. Assess the fabric after finishing and after relevant washing cycles.","Key parameters include GSM, usable width, skew and bow, shrinkage, shade, stretch and recovery, crocking, and seam strength. Do not use a trade description such as “heavy denim” as a substitute for measurable specifications.","**Design rationale and scope.** Denim is commonly identified by its woven twill construction, in which the warp is often indigo-dyed and the weft may remain lighter. However, construction and formulation vary by product. Wear characteristics depend on yarn count, density, weave, fibre composition, slub, stretch yarns, weight, and finishing; a blue surface alone does not describe denim quality.","**Implementation process and control records.** Review fabric specifications for composition, weave, GSM, width, shade, shrinkage, skew and bow, stretch, and recovery where applicable. Inspect the face and back, grain direction, slub consistency, and weaving defects. For stretch denim, identify the stretch direction and dimensional changes after washing. Sample the beginning, middle, and end of rolls, as well as the selvedge and centre, to assess variation.","**Case analysis and verification.** If two rolls with the same code differ in hand feel or colour, investigate dye lot, construction, finish, and washing conditions. Do not assess shade under ordinary room lighting alone. Markers and patterns must account for shrinkage, skew, and fabric direction; ignoring these factors can cause twisted side seams and an unbalanced garment after washing."],"deep":"Denim commonly uses indigo-dyed warp yarns and lighter weft yarns in a twill construction, but construction and fibre content vary with the product objective. Check weight, usable width, shrinkage, skew, colourfastness, and suitability for the intended wash effect. For stretch denim, assess elastane content, recovery, and dimensional change after washing.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Denim is commonly identified by its woven twill construction, in which the warp is often indigo-dyed and the weft may remain lighter. However, construction and formulation vary by product. Wear characteristics depend on yarn count, density, weave, fibre composition, slub, stretch yarns, weight, and finishing; a blue surface alone does not describe denim quality.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Review fabric specifications for composition, weave, GSM, width, shade, shrinkage, skew and bow, stretch, and recovery where applicable. Inspect the face and back, grain direction, slub consistency, and weaving defects. For stretch denim, identify the stretch direction and dimensional changes after washing. Sample the beginning, middle, and end of rolls, as well as the selvedge and centre, to assess variation.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** If two rolls with the same code differ in hand feel or colour, investigate dye lot, construction, finish, and washing conditions. Do not assess shade under ordinary room lighting alone. Markers and patterns must account for shrinkage, skew, and fabric direction; ignoring these factors can cause twisted side seams and an unbalanced garment after washing.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a use case–risk–construction–test matrix for a product in this group. Record the market, user, conditions of use, required documents, sample configuration, and evidence needed before approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Denim: Fabric Structure and Raw-Material Characteristics\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Denim: Fabric Structure and Raw-Material Characteristics</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Denim: Fabric Structure and Raw-Material Characteristics</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Denim Structure</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Washing / Finishing</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Shade and Durability</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Lot Approval</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"4":{"title":"Denim Washing: Enzyme, Stone, and Fading Effects","cat":"Denim","body":["Wash effects may be produced using enzymes, abrasive stones, oxidizing chemicals, ozone, laser processing, or combinations of these methods. Each process creates a different contrast and affects the material differently; the same recipe may produce different results across fabric lots.","Controls must cover pre- and post-wash shade, tensile and tear strength, weight loss, seam strength, post-treatment pH, and garment-to-garment consistency. Excessive abrasion can weaken pocket areas, the seat, thighs, or seams.","Do not apply one general recipe to every denim fabric. Process selection must be based on fibre composition, dye system, machine, chemicals, SDS/TDS information, and trials. Ozone, permanganate, and oxidizing chemicals require controls for worker safety, emissions, and wastewater.","**Design rationale and scope.** Enzyme washing, stone washing, and fading effects act on the denim surface through different mechanisms. Cellulase enzymes act on surface cellulose; abrasive stones create mechanical impact; fading may involve chemical treatment, mechanical abrasion, or a combination. Results depend on the fabric base, dye, pH, temperature, time, machine load, and neutralization process.","**Implementation process and control records.** Develop the wash using a controlled trial matrix covering fabric code and lot, load ratio, chemicals, operating conditions specified by suppliers, processing time, stopping criteria, and post-wash treatment. Record pre- and post-wash shade, strength, hand feel, seam strength, surface damage, and variation between samples. Conduct small-scale trials before scaling up, and define appearance criteria using an approved physical standard.","**Case analysis and verification.** If the effect is uneven, check machine loading, stone distribution, contact level, sample ratio, and lot differences. If the fabric becomes weak or tears, assess cumulative process impact and stress-concentration points. Do not copy a recipe from another denim base; changes in composition or dye system can significantly alter the washing result."],"deep":"Enzyme washing acts on surface cellulose; stone washing creates mechanical abrasion; fading effects may result from several combined processes. Control machine load, garment ratio, chemicals, time, temperature, and process sequence according to a validated recipe. Evaluate contrast, fibre damage, seam strength, shade, and repeatability between batches.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Enzyme washing, stone washing, and fading effects act on the denim surface through different mechanisms. Cellulase enzymes act on surface cellulose; abrasive stones create mechanical impact; fading may involve chemical treatment, mechanical abrasion, or a combination. Results depend on the fabric base, dye, pH, temperature, time, machine load, and neutralization process.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Develop the wash using a controlled trial matrix covering fabric code and lot, load ratio, chemicals, operating conditions specified by suppliers, processing time, stopping criteria, and post-wash treatment. Record pre- and post-wash shade, strength, hand feel, seam strength, surface damage, and variation between samples. Conduct small-scale trials before scaling up, and define appearance criteria using an approved physical standard.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** If the effect is uneven, check machine loading, stone distribution, contact level, sample ratio, and lot differences. If the fabric becomes weak or tears, assess cumulative process impact and stress-concentration points. Do not copy a recipe from another denim base; changes in composition or dye system can significantly alter the washing result.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a use case–risk–construction–test matrix for a product in this group. Record the market, user, conditions of use, required documents, sample configuration, and evidence needed before approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Denim Washing: Enzyme, Stone, and Fading Effects\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Denim Washing: Enzyme, Stone, and Fading Effects</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Denim Washing: Enzyme, Stone, and Fading Effects</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Denim Structure</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Washing / Finishing</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Shade and Durability</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Lot Approval</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"5":{"title":"Denim Garment Finishing and Shade Control","cat":"Denim","body":["Denim shade varies with fabric lot, cutting direction, position along the roll, washing process, and garment placement in the machine. For directional panels, reversing the cutting direction or combining panels from different lots can create visible colour differences.","Establish shade grouping, an approved post-wash standard, panel-matching rules, and checks on garments from the beginning, middle, and end of a lot. Details such as pockets, waistbands, and front panels must be assessed under the same lighting conditions.","Evaluate both appearance and durability. A wash effect may look attractive but still be unacceptable if it reduces strength or causes staining on contrasting materials.","**Design rationale and scope.** Denim garment finishing must control shade, hand feel, dimensions, and strength after washing. Indigo colour can change through abrasion and processing. Shade variation between panels or garments may be an intentional design effect or a defect, depending on whether it was defined in advance.","**Implementation process and control records.** Develop a wash standard using photographs and physical samples for each relevant area. Define whiskering, abrasion, tint, resin, or softener effects where applicable. Assess shade under agreed lighting conditions and with the garment positioned consistently. Check post-wash dimensions, twist and skew, tensile and tear strength, seams, and trims. Retain the recipe, lot, machine, load, processing date, and control sample.","**Case analysis and verification.** If shade differs between panels, trace the fabric lot, cutting plan, roll position, and washing process. Distinguish panel-to-panel variation from intentional abrasion effects. Do not use a phone photograph as the sole standard because white balance and lighting vary; use an approved physical standard and a defined assessment procedure."],"deep":"Garment finishing takes place after sewing and can change the dimensions, colour, strength, and appearance of the completed product. Establish shade grouping by visible area, panel, or individual garment according to the requirement. Use an approved standard, consistent lighting conditions, and lot-classification rules. Monitor effects on labels, trims, and seams.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Denim garment finishing must control shade, hand feel, dimensions, and strength after washing. Indigo colour can change through abrasion and processing. Shade variation between panels or garments may be an intentional design effect or a defect, depending on whether it was defined in advance.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Develop a wash standard using photographs and physical samples for each relevant area. Define whiskering, abrasion, tint, resin, or softener effects where applicable. Assess shade under agreed lighting conditions and with the garment positioned consistently. Check post-wash dimensions, twist and skew, tensile and tear strength, seams, and trims. Retain the recipe, lot, machine, load, processing date, and control sample.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** If shade differs between panels, trace the fabric lot, cutting plan, roll position, and washing process. Distinguish panel-to-panel variation from intentional abrasion effects. Do not use a phone photograph as the sole standard because white balance and lighting vary; use an approved physical standard and a defined assessment procedure.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a use case–risk–construction–test matrix for a product in this group. Record the market, user, conditions of use, required documents, sample configuration, and evidence needed before approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Denim Garment Finishing and Shade Control\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Denim Garment Finishing and Shade Control</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Denim Garment Finishing and Shade Control</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Denim Structure</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Washing / Finishing</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Shade and Durability</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Lot Approval</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"6":{"title":"Denim Trousers: Construction and Stress Points","cat":"Denim","body":["Denim trousers have several load-bearing areas: the seat, crotch, pockets, belt loops, fly, pocket openings, and bartack points. Seam construction, seam allowance, thread, SPI, and reinforcement must suit the fabric weight and intended use.","Assess seams while the wearer squats, takes long steps, sits, and bends. Distinguish fit-related tension from construction or material failures.","Test seam strength, seam slippage, zippers, buttons, belt-loop pull, and durability after washing according to product risks. Test specimens must represent the actual bulk construction.","**Design rationale and scope.** Denim trousers experience concentrated loads at the seat, crotch, pockets, waistband, belt loops, and seam intersections. Traditional five-pocket construction contains multiple layers at pocket openings, bartacks, and side seams. Design must balance durability, bulk, seated comfort, and appearance after washing.","**Implementation process and control records.** Review the pattern in standing and seated postures, including front and back crotch curves, rise, thigh width, and knee opening. Specify bartack, rivet, reinforcement, and thread type based on testing. Check assembly sequence, thickness at intersections, sewing capability through multiple layers, and thread-trimming positions. Conduct pull tests at stress points and wear trials for the intended activities.","**Case analysis and verification.** If the crotch tears, distinguish fit-related tensile loading, inadequate seam strength, fabric weakened by washing, and pattern errors. If a pocket tears, check load direction, bartack, and edge distance. Do not simply increase stitch density, as this may perforate the fabric or create a hard area; the solution must address the actual failure mode."],"deep":"Denim trousers carry concentrated loads at the seat, crotch, pockets, belt loops, and seam ends. Specify seam type, stitch density, bartacks, rivets, and reinforcement layers by location. Check seam slippage, fabric tearing, trim detachment, and freedom of movement. Avoid adding reinforcement indiscriminately if it creates bulk or discomfort.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Denim trousers experience concentrated loads at the seat, crotch, pockets, waistband, belt loops, and seam intersections. Traditional five-pocket construction contains multiple layers at pocket openings, bartacks, and side seams. Design must balance durability, bulk, seated comfort, and appearance after washing.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Review the pattern in standing and seated postures, including front and back crotch curves, rise, thigh width, and knee opening. Specify bartack, rivet, reinforcement, and thread type based on testing. Check assembly sequence, thickness at intersections, sewing capability through multiple layers, and thread-trimming positions. Conduct pull tests at stress points and wear trials for the intended activities.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** If the crotch tears, distinguish fit-related tensile loading, inadequate seam strength, fabric weakened by washing, and pattern errors. If a pocket tears, check load direction, bartack, and edge distance. Do not simply increase stitch density, as this may perforate the fabric or create a hard area; the solution must address the actual failure mode.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a use case–risk–construction–test matrix for a product in this group. Record the market, user, conditions of use, required documents, sample configuration, and evidence needed before approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Denim Trousers: Construction and Stress Points\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Denim Trousers: Construction and Stress Points</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Denim Trousers: Construction and Stress Points</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Denim Structure</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Washing / Finishing</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Shade and Durability</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Lot Approval</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"7":{"title":"Sportswear: Movement Requirements and Moisture Management","cat":"Sportswear","body":["Sportswear must be assessed against exercise intensity, heat generation, perspiration, skin friction, stretch, and washing frequency. A fabric that absorbs water quickly does not necessarily dry quickly; wicking and drying are separate properties.","Design considerations include ventilation zones, low-friction seams, multidirectional stretch, storage pockets, and stability during movement. Multiple fabrics may be combined in a panelled construction, but their stretch compatibility must be checked.","Evaluate the material and complete garment after washing, stretching, abrasion, and wear trials. A test result on fabric alone is not sufficient to claim that the complete garment manages moisture effectively.","**Design rationale and scope.** Sportswear must support movement, thermal and moisture management, and repeated contact with the skin. Moisture transport depends not only on fibre composition but also on fabric construction, finishing, fit, contact area, and base layers. High-intensity sportswear has different requirements from walking apparel or everyday clothing.","**Implementation process and control records.** Identify the sport, movements, intensity, duration, perspiration zones, and climate. Select materials based on stretch, recovery, abrasion, pilling, drying, and skin-contact comfort. Position seams away from friction zones, provide ventilation where appropriate, and assess the garment during movement. Wear trials should record size, weather, activity, duration, and user feedback against a defined scale.","**Case analysis and verification.** If a garment clings to the body, investigate fit, fabric construction, and accumulated moisture. If seams cause chafing, assess seam position, bulk, and stitch type. Do not infer moisture-management performance from fibre-content labels alone; use material data and garment-level evaluation in the intended use case."],"deep":"Sportswear requires a coordinated balance of moisture transport, quick drying, stretch, ventilation, and stability during movement. Individual fabric test results do not fully describe the wearing experience because seams, linings, and fit also have an effect. Identify the sport, intensity, duration, and perspiration zones; test on the body during a relevant activity and record the test conditions.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Sportswear must support movement, thermal and moisture management, and repeated contact with the skin. Moisture transport depends not only on fibre composition but also on fabric construction, finishing, fit, contact area, and base layers. High-intensity sportswear has different requirements from walking apparel or everyday clothing.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Identify the sport, movements, intensity, duration, perspiration zones, and climate. Select materials based on stretch, recovery, abrasion, pilling, drying, and skin-contact comfort. Position seams away from friction zones, provide ventilation where appropriate, and assess the garment during movement. Wear trials should record size, weather, activity, duration, and user feedback against a defined scale.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** If a garment clings to the body, investigate fit, fabric construction, and accumulated moisture. If seams cause chafing, assess seam position, bulk, and stitch type. Do not infer moisture-management performance from fibre-content labels alone; use material data and garment-level evaluation in the intended use case.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a use case–risk–construction–test matrix for a product in this group. Record the market, user, conditions of use, required documents, sample configuration, and evidence needed before approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Sportswear: Movement Requirements and Moisture Management\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Sportswear: Movement Requirements and Moisture Management</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Sportswear: Movement Requirements and Moisture Management</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Activity</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Materials and Fit</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Seam Engineering</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Wear Trial</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"8":{"title":"Leggings and Compression Garments","cat":"Sportswear","body":["Leggings require control of coverage under extension, opacity, stretch and recovery, growth, seam strength, and waistband stability. A dark colour or heavy fabric does not automatically prevent show-through during a squat.","Pattern design must account for material stretch in both directions, seam placement, gusset, rise, and force distribution. Compression garments have a different purpose and different criteria from ordinary fashion leggings.","Test representative sizes and wearers in squats, lunges, sitting, and movement. Measure opacity using a defined method and assess the garment after washing.","**Design rationale and scope.** Leggings and compression garments apply contact pressure to the body. Pressure distribution depends on the pattern, material stretch and recovery, body dimensions, garment dimensions, and seams. Compression does not mean maximum tightness. Excessive or uneven pressure can cause discomfort, restrict movement, or leave pressure marks.","**Implementation process and control records.** Define the intended use and designed compression level. If medical or therapeutic claims are made, require a separate assessment against the relevant specialist compliance requirements. Check directional stretch and recovery, dimensional stability after washing, seam slippage, and coverage under extension. Fit-test across a representative range of body sizes, recording pressure points, rolling edges, show-through, and changes during squats and bending.","**Case analysis and verification.** If the waistband rolls, do not simply increase elastic tension; review waistband proportions, height, curvature, friction, and shrinkage after washing. If seams fail, check stitch extensibility, thread, needle, stitch density, and panel construction. Use compression claims only when supported by evidence appropriate to the claim and design scope."],"deep":"Compression garments should distribute pressure by body zone and maintain fit through repeated wear and washing. Design should consider fabric construction, stretch direction, negative ease, and transition zones. Check tensile force, recovery, deformation, edge rolling, and pressure marks on test wearers. Do not assign a medical pressure classification unless the product has been designed and verified against the corresponding requirements.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Leggings and compression garments apply contact pressure to the body. Pressure distribution depends on the pattern, material stretch and recovery, body dimensions, garment dimensions, and seams. Compression does not mean maximum tightness. Excessive or uneven pressure can cause discomfort, restrict movement, or leave pressure marks.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define the intended use and designed compression level. If medical or therapeutic claims are made, require a separate assessment against the relevant specialist compliance requirements. Check directional stretch and recovery, dimensional stability after washing, seam slippage, and coverage under extension. Fit-test across a representative range of body sizes, recording pressure points, rolling edges, show-through, and changes during squats and bending.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** If the waistband rolls, do not simply increase elastic tension; review waistband proportions, height, curvature, friction, and shrinkage after washing. If seams fail, check stitch extensibility, thread, needle, stitch density, and panel construction. Use compression claims only when supported by evidence appropriate to the claim and design scope.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a use case–risk–construction–test matrix for a product in this group. Record the market, user, conditions of use, required documents, sample configuration, and evidence needed before approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Leggings and Compression Garments\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Leggings and Compression Garments</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Leggings and Compression Garments</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Activity</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Materials and Fit</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Seam Engineering</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Wear Trial</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"9":{"title":"Sportswear: Raglan Sleeves, Gussets, and Seam Engineering","cat":"Sportswear","body":["Raglan sleeves, underarm gussets, and shaped panels can improve movement range or redistribute seam lines. However, every additional seam also creates a potential friction point, increases sewing time, and introduces another opportunity for defects.","Select flatlock, coverstitch, overlock, or another seam construction according to fabric thickness, stretch, and skin-contact area. Do not assume that flatlock is always the best option; validate the construction on the actual material.","Evaluate arm movement, torso rotation, chafing, seam grin, and durability. Inspect the inside of the garment as well, because a seam may feel smooth in one area but create a pressure point at an intersection.","**Design rationale and scope.** A raglan sleeve changes the sleeve-to-shoulder seam and may improve movement range. A gusset adds volume or redistributes load in the underarm or crotch area. Seam engineering selects seam position, type, and construction to reduce friction or improve strength. Performance depends on pattern, material, stretch direction, and actual movement.","**Implementation process and control records.** Assess arm raising, torso rotation, reaching, and bending. Check whether seams cross friction zones or load points, and define the required stretch direction and panel orientation. Select flatlock, coverstitch, overlock, or another seam according to thickness, stretch, appearance, and strength requirements. Make seam coupons and garment mock-ups before locking the construction.","**Case analysis and verification.** If the sleeve pulls the body upwards, check raglan length, gusset, armhole depth, and panel stretch. If a seam chafes the skin, assess bulk, thread ends, and seam position. Do not assume flatlock is always superior; validate the seam type against the material and movement conditions."],"deep":"A raglan sleeve changes the shoulder seam to support movement, while a gusset can increase range of motion and reduce tension at the underarm or crotch. Seam engineering selects flat seams, coverstitch, bonded seams, or other constructions according to thickness and stretch. Test extension during movement, skin friction, and deformation after washing. Where possible, keep seams away from high-friction areas.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** A raglan sleeve changes the sleeve-to-shoulder seam and may improve movement range. A gusset adds volume or redistributes load in the underarm or crotch area. Seam engineering selects seam position, type, and construction to reduce friction or improve strength. Performance depends on pattern, material, stretch direction, and actual movement.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Assess arm raising, torso rotation, reaching, and bending. Check whether seams cross friction zones or load points, and define the required stretch direction and panel orientation. Select flatlock, coverstitch, overlock, or another seam according to thickness, stretch, appearance, and strength requirements. Make seam coupons and garment mock-ups before locking the construction.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** If the sleeve pulls the body upwards, check raglan length, gusset, armhole depth, and panel stretch. If a seam chafes the skin, assess bulk, thread ends, and seam position. Do not assume flatlock is always superior; validate the seam type against the material and movement conditions.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a use case–risk–construction–test matrix for a product in this group. Record the market, user, conditions of use, required documents, sample configuration, and evidence needed before approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Sportswear: Raglan Sleeves, Gussets, and Seam Engineering\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Sportswear: Raglan Sleeves, Gussets, and Seam Engineering</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Sportswear: Raglan Sleeves, Gussets, and Seam Engineering</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Activity</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Materials and Fit</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Seam Engineering</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Wear Trial</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"10":{"title":"Swimwear and Materials Exposed to Chlorine and Seawater","cat":"Sportswear","body":["Swimwear materials must retain elasticity in water, withstand chlorine or seawater according to the use case, dry appropriately, and maintain colour. Nylon/elastane and polyester/elastane behave differently under UV exposure, chlorine, heat, and washing.","Pattern design must control coverage, lining, elastic, leg openings, straps, and stability when wet. The tension in straps and elastics can change substantially as materials age.","Test colourfastness, stretch and recovery, chlorine resistance where required, dimensional stability, and seam performance. Use care instructions appropriate to the material, and do not generalize a chlorine test result to every pool chemical.","**Design rationale and scope.** Swimwear is exposed to chlorine, salt, UV radiation, sunscreen, and repeated wet-dry cycles. Elastane may degrade under environmental exposure and care conditions. Dyes, linings, thread, and trims may also fade, become brittle, or lose elasticity. Construction must maintain coverage, fit, and stability when wet.","**Implementation process and control records.** Define the use profile: chlorinated pool, seawater, recreational swimming, or competition. Select materials according to colourfastness, stretch and recovery, wet stability, UV requirements, and skin-contact requirements. Assess samples after representative exposure, and test seams, lining, straps, waistband, and attachment points. Care instructions should reflect rinsing, drying, and heat-avoidance requirements where these are supported by the selected material.","**Case analysis and verification.** If swimwear loses shape after a few uses, investigate elastane, heat, chlorine, exposure time, and care practices. If the lining twists or becomes exposed, check relative shrinkage, pattern, and anchoring points. Do not use new-fabric test results to claim service life without ageing tests or other relevant evidence."],"deep":"Swimwear is exposed to chlorine, salt, sunlight, and tensile loads when wet. Assess materials for colour retention, recovery, dimensional stability, and wet comfort; check that lining, elastic, and trims are compatible with these conditions. Evaluate fit both wet and dry. Define rinsing and drying instructions based on the selected materials.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Swimwear is exposed to chlorine, salt, UV radiation, sunscreen, and repeated wet-dry cycles. Elastane may degrade under environmental exposure and care conditions. Dyes, linings, thread, and trims may also fade, become brittle, or lose elasticity. Construction must maintain coverage, fit, and stability when wet.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define the use profile: chlorinated pool, seawater, recreational swimming, or competition. Select materials according to colourfastness, stretch and recovery, wet stability, UV requirements, and skin-contact requirements. Assess samples after representative exposure, and test seams, lining, straps, waistband, and attachment points. Care instructions should reflect rinsing, drying, and heat-avoidance requirements where these are supported by the selected material.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** If swimwear loses shape after a few uses, investigate elastane, heat, chlorine, exposure time, and care practices. If the lining twists or becomes exposed, check relative shrinkage, pattern, and anchoring points. Do not use new-fabric test results to claim service life without ageing tests or other relevant evidence.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a use case–risk–construction–test matrix for a product in this group. Record the market, user, conditions of use, required documents, sample configuration, and evidence needed before approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Swimwear and Materials Exposed to Chlorine and Seawater\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Swimwear and Materials Exposed to Chlorine and Seawater</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Swimwear and Materials Exposed to Chlorine and Seawater</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Activity</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Materials and Fit</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Seam Engineering</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Wear Trial</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"11":{"title":"Rain Jackets: Distinguishing Water-Resistant from Waterproof","cat":"Outerwear","body":["Water-resistant generally describes a limited ability to resist water under defined conditions. Waterproof requires explicit criteria and a specified test method. A spray rating is not equivalent to hydrostatic head and does not establish that an entire garment is watertight.","Assess the fabric, coating or membrane, seam construction, zippers, pockets, hood, cuffs, and junctions between components. Water often enters through seams, closures, and pressure-exposed areas before it penetrates the face fabric.","Define rainfall conditions, exposure duration, water pressure, movement, and acceptance criteria. Test the complete garment after laundering and flexing; testing a fabric swatch alone is insufficient.","**Design basis and scope.** Water-resistant and waterproof describe different levels of performance. A finished jacket’s resistance to water depends on the fabric, membrane or coating, seams, closures, storm flaps, cuffs, hood, and openings. A hydrostatic-head result or spray rating for the fabric does not, by itself, demonstrate that the garment remains waterproof during prolonged rain.","**Implementation process and control records.** Define the product claim, rainfall conditions, exposure time, water pressure, and target market. Review material specifications, seam sealing, seam tape, zippers, pockets, and hood construction. Plan tests on both materials and the finished garment using the required methods; record leakage locations, test conditions, and sample configuration. Control the temperature, pressure, and dwell time used to apply seam tape in accordance with the equipment and material suppliers’ instructions.","**Case analysis and verification.** If leakage occurs at a seam, inspect tape width, overlap, intersections, and bonding surfaces. If water enters at a zipper, review the zipper construction and storm flap. Do not make a waterproof claim based only on fabric-level water-resistance data; the claim must match the tested construction and actual product configuration."],"deep":"Water resistance is a bounded performance description, whereas a waterproof claim requires a suitable construction and supporting test evidence. Assess the face fabric, coating or membrane, seams, closures, storm flaps, and openings as one system. Water may enter through a seam or cuff even when the base fabric passes its own test. Record the test method and exact product configuration used to substantiate the claim.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** The finished jacket’s water resistance depends on the interaction of the fabric, membrane or coating, seam sealing, zipper, hood, cuffs, and openings. Fabric-level hydrostatic-head and spray results cannot independently establish whole-garment performance.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define the intended weather exposure and acceptance criteria. Verify seam-tape application settings and inspect high-risk intersections, closures, and pocket entries. Test the complete garment after relevant care and flexing conditions.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** Trace leakage to the actual entry point before changing materials. Distinguish fabric penetration from seam, zipper, and opening leakage. Keep product claims within the scope of the applicable test evidence.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a use-case, risk, construction, and test matrix for a rain jacket. Specify the exposure conditions, garment configuration, test methods, leakage records, and evidence required for the proposed claim.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Rain Jackets: Distinguishing Water-Resistant from Waterproof\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Rain Jackets: Distinguishing Water-Resistant from Waterproof</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Rain Jackets: Distinguishing Water-Resistant from Waterproof</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Weather conditions</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Shell materials</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Seams / closures</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Garment test</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"12":{"title":"Layered Jackets and the Layering System","cat":"Outerwear","body":["Layering commonly separates functions into a next-to-skin base layer, an insulating layer, and an outer weather-protection layer. The layers must work together in terms of breathability, moisture-vapor transport, drape, friction, and freedom of movement.","Total thickness, interlayer slippage, seam placement, and sleeve and armhole dimensions affect how garments can be worn together. An outer layer that is too tight can compress insulation and reduce its effectiveness.","Test the complete system for movement, hood and cuff adjustment, zipper operation, and moisture release. Do not add the individual performance ratings of separate layers to infer overall system performance.","**Design basis and scope.** A layering system distributes functions among a base layer, an insulating layer, and an outer protective layer. Its effectiveness depends on trapped air, moisture transport, activity level, and adjustability. Adding layers does not automatically improve warmth or comfort if the combination restricts movement or retains moisture.","**Implementation process and control records.** Define the environment and expected activity level, then assign each layer its role in moisture management, insulation, wind or rain protection, and durability. Assess dimensional compatibility, slippage, friction, bulk, and access to closures across the layers. Test the garments as a combination rather than testing each item only in isolation. Record the clothing combination, activity, and weather conditions used during evaluation.","**Case analysis and verification.** If the wearer feels cold despite an effective outer shell, examine moisture in the base layer, trapped-air volume, fit, and ventilation. If the wearer overheats, review adjustment features and activity level. Do not infer system performance from the specification of a single layer."],"deep":"A layering system combines a moisture-managing base layer, an insulating middle layer, and an outer layer that protects against weather. The layers must be compatible in width, length, surface friction, and vapor release. A garment that performs well on its own may still restrict movement when worn with the other layers. Inspect the neck, cuffs, hem, and interfaces between garments.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** System performance depends on the interaction of insulation, moisture transport, ventilation, fit, and the air space between layers. Excessive compression can reduce insulation, while poor moisture management can leave the wearer damp.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Establish realistic clothing combinations and activity scenarios. Check sleeve reach, armhole mobility, hem coverage, closure access, and adjustment range with all layers worn together. Record the combination and conditions used in wear trials.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** Investigate cold or overheating complaints across the whole clothing system rather than attributing them automatically to the shell fabric. Separate material properties from fit, ventilation, and activity effects.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Build a layered-outfit evaluation plan that records each layer’s function, dimensional compatibility, movement checks, moisture observations, and intended weather range.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Layered Jackets and the Layering System\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Layered Jackets and the Layering System</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Layered Jackets and the Layering System</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Base layer</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Insulation</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Outer shell</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">System trial</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"13":{"title":"Softshell, Hardshell, and Membrane Construction","cat":"Outerwear","body":["Softshell products generally prioritize softness, stretch, and comfort, while hardshell products generally prioritize a weather barrier. Commercial labels are not test standards; the actual construction and performance data must be reviewed.","Membranes may be microporous or hydrophilic, depending on the technology. Hydrostatic head, MVTR or RET, air permeability, flex durability, and abrasion resistance must be considered together.","Check durability after laundering, abrasion, flexing, and seam sealing, as well as changes in hand feel. Coatings and membranes can be damaged by heat, laundry chemicals, or incompatible fabric softeners.","**Design basis and scope.** Softshell constructions commonly emphasize a soft hand, stretch, and a designed level of wind or water resistance. Hardshell constructions prioritize a weather barrier through fabric, membrane, or coating structures. A membrane may be a separate laminated layer or part of another supplier-defined construction. A trade name does not replace a description of the actual layers and their performance data.","**Implementation process and control records.** Review the face fabric, membrane or coating, backing, adhesive, and seams. Identify the properties that need verification, including water resistance, vapor transmission, wind resistance, stretch, abrasion, hydrolysis resistance, and interlayer bond strength. Assess the material after the specified care process and inspect for peeling, cracking, delamination, and changes in hand feel. Compare the construction with the intended use and care conditions.","**Case analysis and verification.** If a membrane delaminates, investigate heat exposure, laundry chemicals, moisture, material age, and lamination quality. If the wearer reports poor comfort, distinguish the material’s vapor-transmission properties from garment ventilation and activity level. Do not use a single MVTR or hydrostatic-head value to conclude that the entire jacket is suitable."],"deep":"Softshell and hardshell describe different design priorities, not universal performance classes. Softshells tend to emphasize flexibility and comfort; hardshells tend to emphasize weather protection. Membrane performance depends on the complete laminate, including face fabric, adhesive, backing, and seam treatment.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Microporous and hydrophilic technologies transport moisture by different mechanisms. Hydrostatic head, MVTR or RET, air permeability, abrasion, flexing, and bond strength describe different aspects of performance and should not be treated as interchangeable.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Verify the supplier’s layer construction and test data. Evaluate laundering, flexing, abrasion, seam sealing, and bonding under the intended care conditions. Track material lots and any changes to coating, adhesive, or finishing chemistry.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** Delamination, cracking, and poor comfort require separate investigations. Consider care conditions and garment ventilation as well as membrane data. Avoid broad suitability claims based on one laboratory value.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Compare a softshell and a hardshell specification against one defined use case. Identify the required properties, trade-offs, test methods, and care-related risks.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Softshell, Hardshell, and Membrane Construction\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Softshell, Hardshell, and Membrane Construction</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Softshell, Hardshell, and Membrane Construction</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use-case needs</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Layer structure</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Performance tests</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Care durability</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"14":{"title":"Underwear: Fit, Comfort, and Material Construction","cat":"Intimates","body":["Underwear remains in prolonged contact with the skin, so softness, seam design, stretch, breathability, dimensional stability, and sensations in sensitive areas require attention. Fiber content is only one factor; fabric construction and finishing strongly influence how a garment feels.","Design development must define coverage, support, elastic placement, lining, and joining points. Elastic force should keep the garment in position without creating excessive pressure or uncomfortable marks.","Conduct fit trials with a representative range of body types, assess garments after laundering, and check elastic recovery, seam strength, colorfastness, and pilling. Protect privacy and obtain informed consent during fitting.","**Design basis and scope.** Underwear is worn close to the skin for extended periods and is exposed to localized pressure, friction, moisture, and repeated movement. Fit must be assessed in relation to body shape, stretch, seams, elastics, labels, and contact zones. Comfort cannot be inferred from the size label alone.","**Implementation process and control records.** Define the size system, fit models, and coverage or support objectives. Evaluate leg openings, waist or chest bands, gusset area, edges, rolling, slippage, and pressure marks after a period of wear. Select materials for recovery, softness, moisture management, colorfastness, and care durability. Conduct post-laundering checks to determine whether elastics and garment dimensions remain stable.","**Case analysis and verification.** If chafing occurs, locate the contact point and inspect the edge, seam, label, or trim. If the garment slips, review fit, force distribution, and surface contact. Increasing elastic tension across the entire garment to correct one slipping area may create excessive pressure elsewhere."],"deep":"Underwear design must account for prolonged skin contact, localized pressure, friction, moisture, and repeated movement. The fiber name alone does not determine comfort; fabric structure, finishing, elastic force, seam bulk, and label placement also matter. Fit assessment should include movement and wear duration.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Coverage, support, edge stability, stretch recovery, softness, and moisture management interact. A garment can match nominal measurements yet still roll, chafe, or leave pressure marks.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define representative fit models and a consistent size system. Record fit observations before and after laundering, including elastic recovery, seam integrity, colorfastness, and pilling. Maintain privacy and informed consent during wearer trials.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** Identify the exact source of discomfort before changing overall tension or dimensions. A localized fit problem should not be corrected by applying excessive compression to the whole garment.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare an underwear fit protocol covering body representation, wear duration, movement, pressure marks, laundering, and material-performance checks.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Underwear: Fit, Comfort, and Material Construction\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Underwear: Fit, Comfort, and Material Construction</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Underwear: Fit, Comfort, and Material Construction</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Body and fit</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Materials</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Seams / contact</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Wear / wash trial</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"15":{"title":"Bra Construction: Cup, Cradle, Wing, and Support Architecture","cat":"Intimates","body":["A bra has a complex structure comprising cups, cradle, wings or band, straps, closure, elastics, and lining. Support depends on the complete design, size, materials, and fit, not simply on cup thickness.","Bra sizing commonly combines band size and cup volume. Grading must consider changes in underbust circumference, bust circumference, cup depth, and strap position together. Not every component should be graded linearly.","Fit assessment should check whether the band remains level, the cups contain the breast tissue, strap pressure is acceptable, underwire placement is correct where applicable, and the garment remains stable during movement. For wired products, inspect wire ends, casing, and the risk of wire penetration.","**Design basis and scope.** The support architecture consists of cups that shape and contain volume, a cradle that provides a supporting base, wings that wrap around the torso, straps that transfer part of the load, and a closure that allows band adjustment. Support results from the interaction of these components, not from shoulder straps or cup thickness alone. Designs vary by intended function, body structure, and activity level.","**Implementation process and control records.** Define the sizing system and measurement method. Check cup volume, underwire line where present, cradle width, wing height, strap placement, closure, and elastic recovery. Conduct fit trials while standing, bending, raising the arms, and performing relevant movements. Record pressure marks, cup overflow or gaping, strap slippage, band movement, and discomfort. Validate grading because cup and band dimensions do not always change in a linear relationship.","**Case analysis and verification.** If straps leave marks, do not simply loosen them; check band stability, cup load distribution, and strap width. If cups wrinkle, review volume, shape, and material behavior. Any support claim must be tied to the intended use and appropriate supporting evidence."],"deep":"Bra support is produced by a coordinated structure. Cups shape and contain volume, the cradle provides a base, wings stabilize the garment around the torso, straps transfer part of the load, and the closure adjusts the band. A single component cannot be assessed independently of the rest of the architecture.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Cup volume and shape, band stability, wing dimensions, strap geometry, elastic recovery, and underwire placement all affect fit and support. Size grading must account for related changes rather than applying one proportional rule to every component.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Use a defined measurement and fitting protocol. Check the garment in static positions and during movement. For wired styles, inspect casing security, wire-end coverage, and penetration risks. Record fit findings by size and style.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** Strap discomfort may originate in poor band support or cup fit. Cup gaping or overflow may reflect volume, shape, or grading. Correct the underlying cause instead of relying on a single adjustment.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Create a bra fit and grading checklist covering cup, cradle, wing, strap, closure, movement, and wire safety where relevant.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bra Construction: Cup, Cradle, Wing, and Support Architecture\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Bra Construction: Cup, Cradle, Wing, and Support Architecture</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Bra Construction: Cup, Cradle, Wing, and Support Architecture</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Cup</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Cradle</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Wing / band</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Strap / closure</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"16":{"title":"Elastic, Lace, and Lightweight Materials in Intimate Apparel","cat":"Intimates","body":["Elastics are available in different constructions and tension levels; equal widths do not guarantee equal recovery force. Lace, mesh, and microfiber also differ in dimensional stability, strength, and needle sensitivity.","Sewing requires control of elastic application stretch, differential feed, stitch type, and joining positions. Excessive stretching distorts the garment, while insufficient tension can leave an edge that does not fit closely.","Test elastic recovery after laundering, seam stretch, snagging, pilling, and colorfastness. Inspect cut edges, pressure points, and the risk of yarn slippage on the finished product.","**Design basis and scope.** Elastic, lace, and lightweight fabrics can have different stretch, recovery, and stability in each direction. Lace may distort at cut edges; elastic may lose force after laundering; and lightweight fabrics can be stretched or damaged by the needle. Joined constructions should avoid abrupt changes in load transfer.","**Implementation process and control records.** Measure stretch and recovery by direction, define an appropriate application tension using the approved internal method, and recheck performance after the specified care cycles. Select needles, thread, stitch type, and presser-foot settings for the lightweight construction; use guides or templates to distribute stretch consistently. For lace, define motif direction and cutting zones. For elastic, record the application ratio and finished length.","**Case analysis and verification.** If an edge ripples, inspect the elastic application ratio, differential feed, thread tension, and material stability. If lace yarns unravel, review the cutting method, stitch density, and load-bearing location. Do not apply one elastic stretch ratio to every elastic type and every garment zone."],"deep":"Elastic, lace, mesh, and microfiber require construction-specific handling. Their stretch and recovery may differ by direction, and their response to needles, feed systems, and laundering can vary significantly. The sewing method must be matched to the material rather than selected by width or appearance alone.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Elastic force and recovery, lace stability, mesh openness, yarn snagging, and lightweight-fabric distortion affect edge quality and fit. Abrupt transitions between materials can concentrate stress.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Record material direction, elastic application ratio, stitch type, needle selection, feed settings, and finished dimensions. Test stretch, recovery, seam extension, snagging, pilling, and laundering performance.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** Rippled edges and unraveling require separate checks of feed, tension, cutting, and material structure. Avoid applying a single sewing or elastic setting across dissimilar materials and zones.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Develop a sewing trial plan for a lace-and-elastic garment, recording material direction, application ratio, machine settings, seam extension, and post-wash results.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Elastic, Lace, and Lightweight Materials in Intimate Apparel\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Elastic, Lace, and Lightweight Materials in Intimate Apparel</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Elastic, Lace, and Lightweight Materials in Intimate Apparel</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Material grain</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Elastic feed</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Seam / edge</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Post-wash recovery</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"17":{"title":"Children’s Garments: Detail Safety and Entanglement Risks","cat":"Childrenwear","body":["Children’s garments must be assessed by age group, body dimensions, ability to dress independently, pulling and mouthing behavior, and use environment. Small components, drawstrings, loops, buttons, and trims can create specific hazards.","Requirements for drawstrings, small parts, attachment strength, chemicals, and flammability vary by market and age group. Consult the current applicable rules rather than relying on one generic checklist.","Check button pull-off strength, trim attachment, seams, labels, sharp edges, and the risk of detached components. Samples for inspection must reflect the actual construction and the sizes presenting the greatest risk.","**Design basis and scope.** Children’s garments require assessment of entanglement, strangulation, ingestion of small parts, sharp edges, detached trims, and incorrect care labeling. Risk depends on age group, mobility, exploratory behavior, and target market. Safety requirements may differ by age and product type.","**Implementation process and control records.** Review every detail, including cords, loops, buttons, zippers, stops, sequins, labels, decorations, and removable parts. Identify risks of catching on equipment or playground structures, wrapping around the neck or limbs, detaching, or becoming a small object. Verify attachment strength, dimensions, placement, and construction against applicable legal and customer requirements. Record the target age and regulatory source in the compliance matrix.","**Case analysis and verification.** A garment should not be considered safe merely because one trim component passed a pull test. Assess the complete product and reasonably foreseeable use conditions. If the requirements for a target market cannot be established, refer the product to the compliance function before release."],"deep":"Children’s garment safety is age- and market-dependent. Review cords, loops, buttons, zippers, decorative parts, labels, sharp edges, and any component that could detach or catch during foreseeable use. The wearer’s age, mobility, behavior, and environment affect the risk assessment.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Small parts, entanglement points, strangulation hazards, sharp edges, and detachable trims require particular attention. A component that appears harmless on a flat sample may create a hazard when worn or pulled.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Maintain a compliance matrix identifying the target age, market, applicable requirements, component checks, attachment tests, and approved sample configuration. Inspect high-risk sizes and complete garments, not only isolated trims.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** A single pull test does not establish overall garment safety. Assess foreseeable use and failure modes, and obtain compliance review when the applicable market requirements are uncertain.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare an age- and market-specific hazard review for a children’s garment, listing each trim, foreseeable misuse, required verification, and release evidence.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Children’s Garments: Detail Safety and Entanglement Risks\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Children’s Garments: Detail Safety and Entanglement Risks</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Children’s Garments: Detail Safety and Entanglement Risks</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Age / use</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Hazard review</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Attachment test</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Compliance</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"18":{"title":"Sleepwear and Market-Specific Flammability Requirements","cat":"Childrenwear","body":["Sleepwear may be subject to specific requirements for flammability, fit, labeling, and age group. A product that meets the requirements in one market may not meet those in another.","Distinguish testing performed on fabric from testing performed on a complete garment. Style, garment looseness, coatings, trims, and care methods can affect the result.","Identify the applicable standard and legislation before design approval. Do not make “flame-resistant” or “fire-safe” claims without testing and an assessment that covers the correct scope.","**Design basis and scope.** Sleepwear may be subject to different flammability requirements depending on the market, age group, product type, and garment classification. Loose or close-fitting construction, material, coatings, and care instructions can affect the risk. A single legal conclusion cannot be applied to every country.","**Implementation process and control records.** Identify the target market, age group, sleepwear type, construction, and current applicable standard or regulation. Review fiber composition, fabric mass per unit area, surface nap, finishes, and trims. Plan flammability testing using the required method and sample configuration; retain certificates, test reports, and corresponding labels or warnings. Reassess approval scope if the material or finish changes.","**Case analysis and verification.** Do not automatically approve a complete sleepwear set using a result obtained on base fabric if the regulation requires a different configuration. Recheck requirements when the product enters another market. A “flame-resistant” claim must be substantiated and must not be interpreted as “fireproof.”"],"deep":"Sleepwear flammability requirements depend on jurisdiction, age group, product classification, fit, and material construction. Requirements for one market cannot be assumed to apply in another. Testing scope must match the actual product and the applicable regulatory method.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Fabric composition, mass, surface nap, fit, coatings, trims, and care conditions can affect flammability behavior. A fabric test and a finished-garment test answer different questions.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Confirm current market requirements before finalizing design and labels. Retain the relevant test reports, certificates, sample configuration, material identification, and approval records. Reassess when materials, finishes, or target markets change.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** Do not extend a fabric-level result beyond its stated scope. Distinguish flame resistance from fireproof performance, and avoid claims that are not supported by the applicable evidence.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Build a market compliance plan for a sleepwear style, identifying age group, fit category, material construction, test scope, labels, and change-control triggers.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Sleepwear and Market-Specific Flammability Requirements\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Sleepwear and Market-Specific Flammability Requirements</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Sleepwear and Market-Specific Flammability Requirements</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Market / age</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Classification</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Flammability test</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Label / approval</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"19":{"title":"Uniforms and Workwear","cat":"Workwear","body":["Workwear must meet the demands of the job, including durability, industrial laundering, identification, and sometimes protective performance. Pocket design, reinforcement, color, and comfort should be based on the occupational task.","Elbows, knees, seat areas, shoulders, and tool pockets may require reinforcement, but reinforcement adds weight and stiffness. The actual load and use conditions must be established before adding it.","Test abrasion resistance, seam strength, colorfastness, dimensional stability, and wear performance. If the garment includes reflective, antistatic, or chemical-resistance claims, verify the relevant requirements on the product.","**Design basis and scope.** Workwear is designed for specific occupational tasks and may need resistance to abrasion, tearing, oils, industrial laundering, heat, or chemical exposure. Design must consider working posture, carried tools, friction zones, visibility, and maintenance. Ordinary workwear is not automatically personal protective equipment (PPE).","**Implementation process and control records.** Conduct a job-task analysis with the employer or wearer: movements, environment, laundering cycles, sharp objects, contact surfaces, and pocket or adjustment needs. Select materials, reinforcements, seams, closures, and pocket positions according to actual loads. If industrial laundering is intended, test after that process and assess shrinkage, shade change, seam strength, and trims.","**Case analysis and verification.** If a pocket tears, determine the carried load, pocket location, reinforcement, and seam construction. If color fades rapidly, investigate laundry chemicals, temperature, abrasion, and dye performance. If the garment carries a protective claim, classify it correctly as PPE where applicable and verify it against the relevant legal requirements."],"deep":"Workwear should be developed from the tasks, postures, environment, and maintenance conditions of the intended job. Identify abrasion zones, load-bearing points, tool pockets, industrial laundering, and visibility needs. Reinforcement can improve durability but may also add weight and stiffness. Confirm pocket placement and movement with the intended users.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Durability requirements may include abrasion, tear resistance, seam strength, dimensional stability, colorfastness, and resistance to the expected cleaning process. Protective functions must be distinguished from ordinary occupational clothing.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Use job-task analysis to define materials, reinforcement, seam construction, pocket loading, closures, and care cycles. Conduct wear trials and post-laundering checks using representative conditions.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** Trace pocket and seam failures to load, placement, and construction. Investigate fading in relation to the actual cleaning chemistry and temperature. Do not imply PPE performance without the applicable classification and evidence.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a workwear specification from a defined job task, including movement, load points, laundering, durability tests, and any protective-claim boundaries.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Uniforms and Workwear\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Uniforms and Workwear</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Uniforms and Workwear</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Job / hazards</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Construction</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Durability test</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use / care</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"20":{"title":"High-Visibility Clothing and Retroreflective Materials","cat":"Workwear","body":["High-visibility clothing uses fluorescent background material and retroreflective material to improve recognition under defined conditions. Fluorescent and retroreflective materials perform different functions.","The area, position, width, and arrangement of reflective bands may be prescribed by the applicable standard and product class. Seams, pockets, logos, or overlays can obscure usable material area.","Check material certification, garment-level area and placement, durability after laundering, and optical performance using the applicable method. Do not design reflective bands by intuition and then attach a standards-compliance claim.","**Design basis and scope.** High-visibility clothing is intended to make the wearer more conspicuous in defined lighting conditions and traffic or worksite environments. Fluorescent background material and retroreflective material serve different functions: fluorescence supports visibility in daylight and certain ambient conditions, while retroreflection returns light toward its source.","**Implementation process and control records.** Identify the environment, risk level, activity, and standard applicable in the target market. Control the area, position, width, spacing, and continuity of reflective tape; avoid obstruction by pockets, straps, or outer layers. Verify the background and retroreflective materials, laundering durability, and finished-garment configuration using the specified methods. Record material lots and approved reference samples.","**Case analysis and verification.** If reflective tape is interrupted at a seam, review the layout and assembly sequence. If performance declines after laundering, trace the number of care cycles and the care instructions. Do not claim a high-visibility class until the applicable standard, garment configuration, and corresponding test results have been established."],"deep":"High-visibility clothing combines fluorescent background material and retroreflective material in a configuration defined by the applicable product standard. Placement must account for usable area, continuity around the torso and limbs, and obstruction when the wearer adds a jacket or equipment. Performance should be assessed against the market’s applicable standard and class. Laundering and abrasion can reduce performance, so the intended care life must be considered.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the garment group.** Fluorescent material and retroreflective material have distinct optical functions. Their effectiveness depends on material properties, area, placement, continuity, condition, and the garment configuration as worn.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Confirm the applicable standard and class before fixing the layout. Control tape width, spacing, placement, interruptions, and coverage. Verify material certification and finished-garment performance, including durability after the specified care process. Maintain lot traceability and approved reference samples.\n\n**Failure investigation and limits of conclusions.** Investigate broken tape paths, hidden areas, and post-wash performance loss against the approved layout and care history. Do not infer compliance from the presence of reflective tape alone.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a high-visibility layout and verification plan for a defined work environment, including standard, class, material area, tape continuity, care cycles, and evidence required for approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"High-Visibility Clothing and Retroreflective Materials\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>High-Visibility Clothing and Retroreflective Materials</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">High-Visibility Clothing and Retroreflective Materials</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical Control Framework for Specialized Product Groups</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Worksite risk</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Hi-vis layout</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Optical / wash test</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Standard approval</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Evaluate the actual product configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"21":{"title":"PPE: Defining Protection Scope and Use Limitations","cat":"Workwear","body":["Personal protective equipment (PPE) is intended to protect against one or more defined hazards. Before design begins, identify the hazard, exposure level, work environment, duration of use, compatibility with other equipment, and applicable product standards.","Protective clothing for chemicals, heat, electric arc, static electricity, cuts, or biological hazards requires different materials, constructions, and test methods. Passing one test does not demonstrate protection against every hazard.","Risk assessment and validation by competent personnel are required. The product documentation must state the protection scope, donning method, storage, cleaning, pre-use inspection, and criteria for retirement.","**Design basis and scope.** PPE requirements must be derived from workplace hazards, exposure levels, tasks, and the consequences of protection failure. Protective clothing must be distinguished from ordinary uniforms and workwear. A garment qualifies as PPE only within the function and configuration that have been assessed.","**Implementation process and control records.** Conduct a hazard assessment with the employer and relevant safety specialists. Identify the body areas requiring protection, exposure routes, duration, environmental conditions, and interactions with gloves, footwear, helmets, respiratory equipment, or other PPE. Select the product category and applicable market standards before choosing materials and construction. Maintain a traceability matrix linking each hazard and requirement to design features, test evidence, instructions, and limitations.","**Case analysis and verification.** A fabric test alone cannot establish the protective performance of a complete garment. Seams, closures, interfaces, coverage, fit, and user behaviour can create failure paths. If the task or hazard changes, reassess the garment rather than assuming the original approval remains valid."],"deep":"PPE selection begins with a defined hazard assessment, not with a generic product label. Record the hazard source, exposure route, intensity, duration, affected body area, work conditions, and consequences of failure. Select materials, seams, closures, coverage, and compatible equipment for that defined scope. Validation must use the applicable standard and the complete configuration where required. Instructions should specify donning, inspection, cleaning, storage, service life, and retirement criteria. A garment must not be used beyond its assessed conditions.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Protective clothing may address chemical splash, heat, flame, electric arc, static discharge, mechanical cutting, biological exposure, or combinations of hazards. These functions rely on different material mechanisms and test procedures. A material that resists one hazard may offer little protection against another. Interfaces, openings, seams, and fit can be as important as the main fabric.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Translate the risk assessment into measurable requirements. Define the target market, standard, product class, size range, coverage, closure design, component specifications, and test plan. Control approved material lots, trims, labels, sewing threads, and construction details. Verify the finished garment and document the configuration represented by each report or certificate. Provide clear user information and a process for inspection, repair, and replacement.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** Investigate failures by tracing the exposure route and the point where protection was lost. Check material degradation, seam opening, closure failure, incorrect fit, damaged components, and incompatibility with other equipment. Do not extend a test result to a different style, material, size range, or hazard without technical justification.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a PPE requirement matrix for one defined workplace task. Record the hazard, exposure, body area, selected product configuration, applicable standard, evidence required, use limitations, and retirement criteria.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"PPE: Defining Protection Scope and Use Limitations\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>PPE: Defining Protection Scope and Use Limitations</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">PPE: Defining Protection Scope and Use Limitations</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Hazard / task</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Protective configuration</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Testing</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use limitations</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"22":{"title":"Antistatic Garments and ESD Control","cat":"Workwear","body":["Electrostatic discharge (ESD) garments are used in environments where static charge must be controlled. Performance depends on conductive fibres, grid or stripe construction, seams, accessories, and the wearer's grounding system.","Surface resistance, point-to-point resistance, and performance retention after laundering are different properties. A garment containing conductive fibres does not by itself ensure that the wearer is correctly grounded.","Evaluate the garment as part of the actual ESD control system and applicable ESD programme. Control changes to fabric, thread, labels, repairs, and the number of laundering cycles.","**Design basis and scope.** ESD garments are intended to control charge accumulation and electrostatic discharge within a defined static-control system. Their effectiveness depends on conductive or dissipative materials, electrical continuity, body coverage, footwear and grounding connections, flooring, humidity, and operating procedures. An ESD coat alone cannot replace the facility's complete control programme.","**Implementation process and control records.** Identify the protected area, sensitivity of products or components, required garment coverage, and the facility's ESD programme. Specify conductive yarn type, grid spacing, fabric construction, seam design, closures, and permitted accessories. Define the relevant electrical measurements and conditioning procedures, including any assessment after laundering. Record garment identification, repair history, cleaning cycles, and replacement criteria.","**Case analysis and verification.** If measured resistance changes after laundering, investigate detergent, softener, contamination, abrasion, and damage to conductive yarns. If the wearer remains charged, check footwear, grounding points, floor, fit, and the complete ESD system. Do not infer system compliance from the garment's material composition alone."],"deep":"ESD garments are components of a controlled electrostatic environment. Specify the required performance from the facility's ESD programme, including garment coverage, conductive or dissipative construction, electrical continuity, laundering method, and verification frequency. Surface resistance and point-to-point resistance describe different electrical characteristics; use the measurement method required by the applicable programme. Confirm that the wearer is grounded through the intended footwear, floor, and grounding arrangements.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Conductive yarns may be arranged as grids or stripes within woven or knitted fabrics. Their spacing, continuity, and condition affect charge control. Seams, closures, labels, embroidery, and repairs can interrupt or alter the intended electrical path. Performance can also vary with humidity, contamination, wear, and cleaning chemistry.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define the ESD zone, user role, garment design, material specification, and test method before sampling. Control conductive yarn placement, panel orientation, seam construction, component substitutions, and approved repair methods. Establish conditioning and laundering procedures representative of actual use. Retain test reports by garment type and lot, and track the number of cleaning cycles where relevant.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** A failed resistance result may arise from damaged conductive yarn, unsuitable laundering, contamination, or a measurement setup that differs from the specified method. Check the garment and the surrounding grounding system before assigning a cause. Do not claim that an ESD garment makes a person or workstation safe without evidence from the complete control system.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Build an ESD garment control plan covering fabric construction, electrical measurements, wearer grounding, laundering, repair, inspection frequency, and replacement criteria.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Antistatic Garments and ESD Control\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Antistatic Garments and ESD Control</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Antistatic Garments and ESD Control</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Hazard / task</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Protective configuration</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Testing</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use limitations</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"23":{"title":"Chemical-Protective Clothing and Barrier Materials","cat":"Workwear","body":["Barrier clothing must be selected for the specific chemical, concentration, temperature, contact duration, and exposure form. Permeation, penetration, and material degradation are different failure mechanisms.","No material is resistant to every chemical. Compatibility must be based on relevant permeation and degradation data, manufacturer guidance, and test conditions representative of the intended exposure.","Design must account for seams, closures, cuffs, hoods, overlaps, and safe doffing. The garment must not be used with chemicals or exposure durations outside its validated scope.","**Design basis and scope.** Chemical barrier materials are selected according to the chemical identity, concentration, temperature, exposure time, physical state, and mechanisms of permeation, penetration, and degradation. Chemical resistance is not an absolute property: a polymer may resist one substance but deteriorate rapidly when exposed to another or to a mixture.","**Implementation process and control records.** Identify the chemicals and mixtures, their concentrations, temperatures, contact conditions, and likely exposure routes. Review manufacturer data and test results for the exact material grade and relevant conditions. Design the complete protective system, including seams, closures, cuff and hood interfaces, overlap dimensions, and doffing sequence. Record the tested configuration, exposure limits, inspection requirements, and replacement rules.","**Case analysis and verification.** If a barrier garment swells, softens, cracks, changes colour, or allows liquid through, stop use and investigate the chemical, temperature, contact time, and material compatibility. Do not transfer data from a single chemical or laboratory condition to an untested mixture or field condition."],"deep":"Chemical-protective clothing must be matched to a defined chemical exposure. Identify each substance or mixture, concentration, temperature, physical state, contact duration, and whether exposure is by splash, spray, immersion, or vapour. Review permeation and degradation data for the exact material and test conditions. Penetration through openings or defects is different from molecular permeation through an intact material. No generic claim of chemical resistance is sufficient for product approval.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Barrier performance depends on polymer chemistry, thickness, coating or laminate integrity, seams, closures, and the condition of the garment. Chemical contact may cause swelling, softening, embrittlement, delamination, or loss of mechanical strength. The weakest interface can determine the protection provided by the complete garment.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Build a chemical compatibility matrix and specify the material grade, garment design, seam sealing method, closure system, and coverage. Define test methods and acceptance criteria for the intended exposure. Validate the complete construction where required, and document limitations such as maximum contact time, permitted chemicals, cleaning restrictions, and inspection intervals. Train users in safe donning and doffing to avoid secondary contamination.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** When leakage or degradation occurs, preserve the failed sample and record the chemical, concentration, temperature, exposure duration, and contact area. Examine seams, closures, surface changes, and material thickness. A result for one chemical, concentration, or temperature must not be extrapolated to another without supporting data.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a chemical compatibility and garment approval matrix for a selected task. Include exposure conditions, barrier material, seam and closure design, test evidence, use limits, inspection, and disposal criteria.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Chemical-Protective Clothing and Barrier Materials\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Chemical-Protective Clothing and Barrier Materials</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Chemical-Protective Clothing and Barrier Materials</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Hazard / task</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Protective configuration</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Testing</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use limitations</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"24":{"title":"Thermal-Protective Jackets and Flame-Resistant Construction","cat":"Workwear","body":["Heat- and flame-protective clothing must be developed for a defined hazard, such as convective heat, radiant heat, direct flame, molten-metal splash, or electric arc. These hazards cannot be represented by a single performance value.","Fabric, thread, closures, labels, linings, and seam construction all affect performance. Components that melt or gaps that expose skin can reduce the protection provided by the garment.","Use the applicable standard and test the complete garment configuration. Laundering, repair, and damage-inspection instructions are part of PPE control.","**Design basis and scope.** Thermal-protective and flame-resistant clothing must be designed for the actual heat source, including contact heat, convection, radiation, sparks, direct flame, or electric arc. Flame resistance of the base fabric does not mean that the wearer is protected against every thermal hazard. Layering, fit, closures, openings, and coverage determine the performance of the complete system.","**Implementation process and control records.** Define the task, heat source, exposure level, duration, movement, and required body coverage. Select the fabric system, lining, thread, fasteners, labels, and reinforcement to suit the hazard. Review the applicable product standard and test plan, then validate the complete garment configuration where required. Document approved materials, construction, care procedures, repair restrictions, and inspection criteria.","**Case analysis and verification.** If a component melts, a seam opens, or skin is exposed through an opening, investigate the component specification, garment fit, closure design, and construction. Do not infer garment-level protection from a fibre name or a fabric-only flame test."],"deep":"Thermal-protective clothing must be specified against the source and severity of the heat hazard. Convective heat, radiant heat, contact heat, flame, molten-metal splash, and electric arc involve different exposure mechanisms and may require different standards and test methods. Flame-resistant fabric alone does not establish the protective performance of a finished jacket.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Protection depends on the full material assembly, including shell, lining, interlining, thread, closures, labels, and reinforcement. Gaps at cuffs, front openings, neck, and hems may expose skin. Components that melt, drip, or lose strength under heat can create additional hazards. Fit must permit movement while maintaining coverage during the task.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define the hazard assessment, target market, product class, body coverage, layering system, and care conditions. Select compatible materials and components, then control their identity and lot traceability. Review seam placement, closure overlap, pocket design, and openings. Test the garment configuration required by the applicable standard and retain reports, labels, instructions, and approved reference samples. Establish procedures for inspection, repair, and retirement after damage or contamination.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** Investigate thermal damage by locating the first point of failure: fabric, seam, closure, component, or coverage gap. Consider whether the garment was worn, layered, cleaned, or repaired as specified. Do not treat “flame-resistant” as synonymous with “fireproof,” and do not extend a test result beyond its stated hazard and configuration.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Develop a thermal hazard-to-design matrix for one work task. Identify the heat source, exposure, garment layers, components, applicable tests, care limits, and inspection or retirement rules.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Thermal-Protective Jackets and Flame-Resistant Construction\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Thermal-Protective Jackets and Flame-Resistant Construction</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Thermal-Protective Jackets and Flame-Resistant Construction</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Hazard / task</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Protective configuration</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Testing</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use limitations</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"25":{"title":"Medical Apparel and Hygiene Control","cat":"Medical and Hygiene","body":["Medical apparel may serve identification, hygiene, or protection against liquids and microorganisms, depending on its product level. An ordinary laboratory coat or uniform is not automatically a protective gown.","Distinguish requirements for laundering durability, particle shedding, barrier performance, seam integrity, and compatibility with disinfection. The required protection must match the exposure type and use procedure.","Test against the product standards and market requirements that apply. Define whether the garment is single-use or reusable. Reusable products must be evaluated after the specified laundering or disinfection cycles.","**Design basis and scope.** Medical and hygiene apparel covers several use levels, including staff uniforms, patient gowns, surgical garments, and products carrying specific barrier claims. Each group has different requirements for fluid exposure, microbial barriers, lint, laundering or disinfection, durability, and comfort during extended wear.","**Implementation process and control records.** Identify the wearer, clinical area, donning and doffing procedure, number of uses, laundering or disinfection method, and type of exposure. Translate these requirements into fabric construction, coverage, closures, seams, fluid resistance, and labelling. Evaluate samples after the intended cleaning cycles and verify dimensions, shade, seam integrity, durability, and barrier performance using appropriate methods.","**Case analysis and verification.** Do not describe an ordinary uniform as a protective gown. Single-use and reusable products require different evidence and control records. A change in disinfection procedure, material, or laundry provider may invalidate the equivalence of a previously assessed configuration."],"deep":"Medical apparel must be developed for a defined clinical or hygiene process. Distinguish staff uniforms, patient clothing, surgical apparel, and garments carrying fluid or microbial barrier claims. Assess coverage, breathability, laundering or disinfection, colour durability, lint generation, and comfort over the intended wear period. Protective claims must be supported by evidence for the specified product configuration.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** The product's role determines the required fabric, seam, closure, and barrier properties. Reusable garments experience repeated cleaning, heat, chemicals, and mechanical action; single-use garments have different handling and disposal requirements. Openings, seam construction, and component attachment may affect barrier integrity and contamination control.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define the clinical area, wearer, exposure type, donning and doffing sequence, cleaning process, and expected service life. Specify material, coverage, seam construction, closure, labels, and packaging. Test the garment according to the applicable product requirements, including after representative laundering or disinfection cycles for reusable products. Maintain traceability for materials, laundry conditions, test results, and approved claims.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** If a garment loses barrier performance, changes dimensions, fades, or sheds fibres, investigate cleaning chemistry, temperature, mechanical action, material ageing, and seam damage. A new-garment test does not establish performance after repeated processing. Reassess the product if the laundry process, material, or intended clinical use changes.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a validation plan for one medical apparel product. Specify its clinical use, exposure, reusable or single-use status, material and seam requirements, cleaning cycles, test evidence, and release criteria.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Medical Apparel and Hygiene Control\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Medical Apparel and Hygiene Control</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Medical Apparel and Hygiene Control</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use process</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Hygiene</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Materials</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Validation</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"26":{"title":"Cleanroom Garments and Particulate Control","cat":"Medical and Hygiene","body":["Cleanroom garments are designed to limit particle and fibre generation and to remain compatible with controlled cleaning procedures. Fabrics, seams, fasteners, labels, and packaging can all become particle sources.","Designs may reduce external pockets, folds, and dust-trapping areas according to the cleanroom class. Materials should be assessed for shedding, particle release, electrostatic behaviour, and cleanability.","Validate the garment against the facility's cleanroom class and operating process. The term 'cleanroom garment' is not a performance guarantee without testing and a defined control system.","**Design basis and scope.** Cleanroom garments are intended to limit the generation, retention, and release of particles from the wearer. Requirements depend on the cleanroom classification, process, product handled, and facility procedures. Particle emission, lint, cleanability, coverage, and donning and doffing practices matter more than the fabric's visual appearance.","**Implementation process and control records.** Confirm the applicable ISO class or internal area requirement, work activity, and reuse cycle. Select filament yarns, fabric construction, closures, cuffs, ankles, and seams appropriate to the process. Define controlled laundering and packaging, then test particle release, linting, or other specified properties. Track material lots, cleaning cycles, repairs, and garment retirement.","**Case analysis and verification.** If particle counts increase after laundering, investigate abrasion, cleaning chemistry, equipment, packaging, and donning procedures. A test report for new fabric does not demonstrate that a garment remains suitable after repeated use; a service-life validation programme is needed."],"deep":"Cleanroom apparel is part of the facility's contamination-control system. Its purpose is to reduce particles and fibres released by the wearer and garment while remaining compatible with the room's cleaning and handling procedures. Requirements depend on the cleanroom class, process sensitivity, product, and facility protocol. A visually clean garment is not evidence of low particle emission.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Fibre type, yarn construction, fabric surface, seams, closures, labels, and packaging can contribute particles. Garment design may limit pockets, exposed edges, folds, and difficult-to-clean features. Electrostatic behaviour and compatibility with the facility's cleaning process may also be important.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Specify the cleanroom classification or internal requirement, wearer activity, garment coverage, reusable life, and controlled laundry process. Select suitable filament fabrics and low-shedding components. Define validated donning, doffing, cleaning, packaging, storage, and transport procedures. Test particle release or linting using the required method, and retain results for the complete garment configuration and relevant use cycles.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** Rising particle counts may result from fabric abrasion, seam damage, unsuitable detergents, laundry equipment, packaging, or poor donning practice. Investigate the full process rather than attributing the result to fabric alone. Revalidate after material, construction, cleaning, or packaging changes. Set a defined retirement point based on evidence and facility requirements.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Create a cleanroom garment control plan covering area classification, material selection, particle testing, laundry, packaging, traceability, repair, and service-life limits.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cleanroom Garments and Particulate Control\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Cleanroom Garments and Particulate Control</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Cleanroom Garments and Particulate Control</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use process</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Hygiene</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Materials</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Validation</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"27":{"title":"Puffer Jackets and Insulation Construction","cat":"Insulated Garments","body":["Puffer jackets may use down, synthetic fill, or other insulation systems. Warmth depends on loft, fill distribution, compartment construction, wind resistance, moisture, and the wearer's activity level.","Down requires control of fill power, fill weight, down-to-feather ratio, cleanliness, and traceability. Synthetic insulation behaves differently when wet, laundered, or compressed.","Check for cold spots, uneven fill, leakage, quilting durability, recovery after compression, and performance after laundering. Evaluate both the shell and lining.","**Design basis and scope.** Puffer jackets retain warmth by creating and maintaining still air within an insulating layer. Performance depends on insulation type, loft, fill distribution, moisture, compartment design, shell fabric, and wearer activity. Down and synthetic insulation differ in weight, recovery, moisture response, and care requirements.","**Implementation process and control records.** Define the expected temperature range, activity, humidity, layering system, compression requirements, and care method. Specify insulation type, fill weight by panel, baffle construction, stitch-through or box-wall design, lining, and shell. Control fill distribution by weighing panels or using a validated production method. Inspect cold spots, leakage, seams, and loft after conditioning.","**Case analysis and verification.** If a jacket has cold areas, examine fill distribution, stitch-through seams, and thermal bridges at construction lines. Do not compare two jackets by fill weight alone; construction, loft, materials, and test conditions also matter. Warmth claims must refer to a defined configuration and use condition."],"deep":"Puffer-jacket performance depends on insulation type, thickness, loft, compartment design, and the distribution of down or synthetic fill. Construction must limit cold spots at quilting lines, joining seams, and compressed areas. Check material migration, loft recovery after compression, leakage, drying behaviour, and performance after care. Thermal performance should be verified on the finished configuration rather than inferred from fill weight alone.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Down insulation is commonly specified by fill power, fill weight, down-to-feather ratio, cleanliness, and traceability. Synthetic fills vary in fibre structure, resilience, moisture response, and wash durability. Baffle geometry and shell permeability affect loft retention and wind protection. Stitch-through construction can create thermal bridges, while box-wall baffles may preserve loft but add complexity and weight.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define the climate, activity, layering, and care profile. Set insulation specifications by panel and control weighing, filling, baffle dimensions, seam allowances, and closure of fill openings. Check shell and lining compatibility, down leakage, stitch integrity, and distribution uniformity. Conduct compression, recovery, laundering, and thermal assessments appropriate to the intended product.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** Cold spots may result from uneven fill, excessive compression, stitch-through lines, or poor fit. Leakage may arise from shell construction, needle damage, seam openings, or unsuitable fabric density. Compare results under equivalent conditioning and test methods. A higher fill weight does not automatically indicate a warmer garment.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a puffer-jacket specification showing insulation type, panel fill weights, baffle design, shell and lining, production checks, care validation, and finished-garment performance evidence.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Puffer Jackets and Insulation Construction\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Puffer Jackets and Insulation Construction</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Puffer Jackets and Insulation Construction</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Insulation</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Baffles / quilting</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Loft retention</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Finished-garment testing</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"28":{"title":"Quilted Garments and Stitch-Line Control","cat":"Insulated Garments","body":["Quilting holds insulation in position but also creates needle lines and potential thermal bridges. Cell dimensions, stitch density, thread type, and material layers affect loft and warmth.","Excessive quilting can reduce loft, while spacing that is too wide may allow fill to migrate. On coated materials, needle holes may also reduce water resistance.","Check quilting uniformity, fill displacement, thread breaks, puckering, needle holes, and changes after laundering. Test samples using the same layer construction as the finished garment.","**Design basis and scope.** Quilting secures insulation layers while creating paths through which heat can transfer. Stitch spacing, cell geometry, stitch density, thread tension, and compression can affect loft, appearance, and durability. With loose insulation, inconsistent quilting may also cause fill migration or local areas with insufficient insulation.","**Implementation process and control records.** Plan the quilting layout by panel, load direction, and movement zone. Specify seam allowance, intersections, stitch density, and sewing sequence. Make trial panels using the actual shell–insulation–lining combination, then assess puckering, cell alignment, flatness, loft, and durability after laundering. Control machine settings, needle, thread, presser-foot pressure, and guides to prevent the upper layer from stretching.","**Case analysis and verification.** When quilt cells distort, measure the deviation in both directions and check differential feed, presser-foot pressure, layer thickness, and assembly sequence. Increasing thread tension is not an appropriate correction when the cause is feeding or panel construction."],"deep":"Quilting keeps insulation in place, but stitch lines can compress the insulating layer and create thermal bridges. Define the spacing, pattern, direction, and start and end points of each quilting line. Check flatness, puckering, layer displacement, and fill distribution after laundering. For large panels, control variation between zones so that appearance and insulation remain consistent.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Stitch-through quilting penetrates the material stack and may compress insulation along every line. Closer spacing improves restraint but can reduce loft; wider spacing may permit fill migration or distortion. Needle size, thread, stitch type, tension, feed balance, and presser-foot pressure influence the finished result. Coated shells may be sensitive to needle perforations.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Establish the quilting map, cell dimensions, tolerances, stitch density, and material stack before bulk production. Validate the process on representative panels and complete garments. Inspect alignment, stitch integrity, puckering, needle damage, loft, and dimensional stability. Record machine settings and approved samples, and confirm performance after the intended care process.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** Distorted cells may be caused by differential feed, excessive pressure, unstable layers, incorrect tension, or an unsuitable sewing sequence. Fill migration may indicate excessive cell size or inadequate restraint. Do not correct every defect by increasing thread tension; identify the mechanism and verify the correction on the complete layer assembly.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Develop a quilting process specification with pattern geometry, cell dimensions, stitch parameters, material stack, inspection points, and post-laundering acceptance criteria.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Quilted Garments and Stitch-Line Control\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Quilted Garments and Stitch-Line Control</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Quilted Garments and Stitch-Line Control</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Insulation</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Baffles / quilting</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Loft retention</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Finished-garment testing</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"29":{"title":"Wind-Resistant Apparel and Ventilation Management","cat":"Outerwear","body":["Wind-resistant fabrics reduce airflow through the material but may also reduce breathability. Design must balance wind protection with heat and moisture release, particularly during physical activity.","Assess air permeability, ventilation locations, zipper flaps, cuffs, hems, and neck closure. A highly sealed garment can cause heat and moisture build-up during high-intensity activity.","Test the product for its intended use case, base layers, and activity level. Fabric wind-resistance data alone cannot establish wearer comfort.","**Design basis and scope.** Wind-resistant garments must limit air exchange through the fabric and openings while managing heat, perspiration, and ventilation. Excessively sealed construction may trap moisture. Adjustable ventilation, such as pit zips, back vents, or opening collars, must be positioned according to posture and activity.","**Implementation process and control records.** Define expected wind speed, activity level, base layers, and any rain-protection requirement. Select shell materials based on air permeability, durability, noise, hand, and compatibility with a membrane or coating where needed. Design adjustable vents, storm flaps, closures, and seams; evaluate the garment on a mannequin or through wear trials in representative movement positions. Record test conditions and the clothing layers used.","**Case analysis and verification.** If the wearer feels cold despite a low-permeability fabric, inspect gaps at the neck, cuffs, and hem, fit, and unintended ventilation. If moisture accumulates, review activity level and vapour release. Wind resistance, waterproofness, and breathability must not be treated as interchangeable properties."],"deep":"Wind-resistant apparel must reduce air movement through both the fabric and garment openings. Gaps at the neck, cuffs, hem, zipper, and hood can undermine the shell's performance. Adjustable ventilation can help regulate heat, but its placement and closure must preserve the intended wind protection. Evaluate the garment in representative wind and activity conditions, record air leakage and moisture build-up, and adjust cords, flaps, or vents based on observed results.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Air permeability describes airflow through a material under specified test conditions; it does not describe every route by which wind enters a garment. Fit, seam construction, closures, and vent design influence the complete system. A membrane or coating may reduce airflow while changing moisture-vapour transport and hand feel.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define wind exposure, activity intensity, expected base layers, and whether rain protection is also required. Specify shell properties, seam treatment, zipper construction, storm flaps, cuff adjustment, hem adjustment, and ventilation options. Conduct wear trials in relevant postures and movement patterns. Record environmental conditions, garment configuration, and wearer feedback alongside laboratory data.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** Cold spots may result from local air leakage, poor fit, or an open vent rather than fabric permeability. Excess moisture may be related to exertion, insufficient ventilation, or limited vapour transfer. Do not use a fabric air-permeability result as a substitute for finished-garment evaluation or as evidence of waterproofness.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a wind-management assessment for one jacket. Map likely air-entry points, ventilation features, shell properties, activity conditions, and the wear-trial observations needed for approval.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Wind-Resistant Apparel and Ventilation Management\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Wind-Resistant Apparel and Ventilation Management</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Wind-Resistant Apparel and Ventilation Management</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Conditions</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">weather</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Material layers</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Seams / closures</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Finished-garment testing</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"30":{"title":"Motorcycle Apparel and Impact-Zone Protection","cat":"Specialized Use","body":["Motorcycle apparel may need to address wind, rain, abrasion, and visibility. Shoulders, elbows, hips, knees, and the back may experience impact or road abrasion depending on the product and riding conditions.","Protectors, reinforcement layers, and seam construction must remain compatible with the riding posture. An ordinary jacket is not automatically certified motorcycle protective clothing.","If the product carries impact or abrasion protection claims, identify the applicable standard and test the correct configuration. Check protector placement, retention during movement, and user instructions.","**Design basis and scope.** Motorcycle apparel is exposed to sliding abrasion, impact, wind, rain, and a forward-flexed riding posture. Shoulders, elbows, back, hips, and knees have different exposure profiles. Protective layers, seam strength, and the ability to keep protectors correctly positioned influence the performance of the complete garment.","**Implementation process and control records.** Define the use case by riding speed, motorcycle type, weather, duration, and protection zones. Specify reinforcement areas and protector pockets, adjustment for seated and standing positions, and compatibility with gloves, helmet, and footwear. Evaluate seam strength, abrasion, tear resistance, closure retention, and protector stability during movement. Protective claims must be linked to the applicable standard and tested configuration.","**Case analysis and verification.** If a protector rotates or shifts when the rider bends, check pocket position, dimensions, straps, and size grading. If a seam opens in an abrasion zone, trace the material, seam type, and applied test load. Fabric abrasion results alone cannot establish the protective performance of the complete riding garment."],"deep":"Motorcycle apparel must be assessed for riding posture, likely road-contact zones, elbows and knees, shoulders, back, and compatibility with helmets, gloves, and footwear. Reinforcement panels and protector pockets must retain their intended position while the rider moves between seated and standing postures. Check seams, material slippage, wind resistance, and comfort during extended riding. Protective claims require evidence from the relevant product tests and standards.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** The garment may combine abrasion-resistant shell materials, impact protectors, reinforcement panels, weather protection, and visibility features. These components address different hazards and must function together. Seam placement, closure security, fit, and protector retention affect coverage during a slide or impact. A component that shifts away from its intended zone may leave the body exposed.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define motorcycle type, riding position, expected speed range, climate, wear duration, and required protection zones. Specify shell and reinforcement materials, seam construction, protector type and location, pocket dimensions, retention systems, and adjustment features. Conduct movement trials in realistic riding positions and verify the complete configuration against the applicable standard. Provide instructions for fitting, inspection, care, and replacement of protectors.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** If a protector moves, assess pocket geometry, garment size, adjustment, and posture. If seams fail, investigate material strength, stitch type, seam allowance, thread, and test loading. If the garment is uncomfortable, assess whether fit changes when the rider leans forward or bends the knees. Do not infer whole-garment protection from an abrasion test on the shell fabric alone.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Create a motorcycle garment design and validation matrix showing riding conditions, exposure zones, shell and reinforcement materials, protector placement, seam requirements, test evidence, and user-care instructions.","diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Motorcycle Apparel and Impact-Zone Protection\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Motorcycle Apparel and Impact-Zone Protection</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Motorcycle Apparel and Impact-Zone Protection</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use case</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Load-bearing zones</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Construction</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Practical testing</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"31":{"title":"Outdoor Apparel and UV Protection","cat":"Specialized Use","body":["Outdoor apparel may be designed for sun, rain, wind, or insect exposure. Each performance claim requires its own verification method; a general outdoor label does not establish a specific level of protection.","Ultraviolet protection depends on fabric construction, colour, moisture, stretch, abrasion, and body coverage. A rating obtained from a flat fabric specimen does not remove the need to assess openings and exposed skin during the intended activity.","Define the target market and applicable labelling rules before making a claim. Verify the relevant material states, garment coverage, and care-related changes, and state the limits of the claim clearly.","**Technical basis and scope.** UV-protective outdoor apparel is commonly evaluated through an Ultraviolet Protection Factor (UPF) or another method required by the target market. Actual protection can change with moisture, extension, wear, colour, fabric structure, and the area covered by the garment. A fabric rating measured in a flat state does not, by itself, establish the protection provided by the garment in motion.","**Development process and records.** Confirm the target market, applicable standard or labelling rule, and proposed claim. Select the fabric construction, colour, density, and any functional finish. Test new material and, where required, material after washing, stretching, or abrasion. Design collars, sleeves, flaps, and openings around the intended activity. Record the specimen, conditioning, test method, and precise scope of the claim.","**Failure analysis and verification.** For highly stretchable products, determine whether the test method requires evaluation in an extended state. Do not transfer a UPF result from one colour or material condition to all variants unless the evidence covers them. Care and use instructions should explain coverage limitations and any additional sun-protection measures."],"deep":"Outdoor apparel may address UV exposure, rain, wind, or insects, and each claim requires its own verification route. UPF performance depends on the material, colour, moisture, extension, and condition during use. Coverage and ventilation must be balanced against the activity and climate. The product specification and label should state the test condition and the limits of the claim.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** UV protection is affected by fibre type, yarn density, knit or woven structure, colour, finishes, and fabric condition. Stretching can open the structure, while moisture and wear may change measured performance. Garment design also matters: necklines, cuffs, sleeve length, hood coverage, and openings determine which skin areas remain exposed. A laboratory result for a fabric specimen should not be interpreted as a guarantee for every body position or use condition.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Identify the target market, intended activity, expected exposure duration, and required coverage. Select representative colours and material constructions, then define whether testing is required before and after washing, stretching, or abrasion. Check garment dimensions and openings in relevant movement positions. Keep the test report linked to the exact material code, colour, finish, lot, and garment configuration. Review label wording against the applicable market rules before release.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** If a garment provides less coverage during movement than expected, review pattern dimensions, sleeve and body length, closure design, and fit grading. If test results differ between colours or lots, investigate construction density, dyeing, finishing, and specimen conditioning. Do not extrapolate one UPF result to untested variants or describe a garment as fully protective when coverage is incomplete.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a UV-protective outdoor garment validation sheet showing the intended activity, target market, material variants, coverage zones, test conditions, care-related checks, claim wording, and evidence required before approval.","labels":["Outdoor Apparel and UV Protection","Outdoor Apparel and UV Protection","Technical control framework for this garment group","Use case","Coverage zones","Construction","Practical testing","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Outdoor Apparel and UV Protection\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Outdoor Apparel and UV Protection</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Outdoor Apparel and UV Protection</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use case</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Load-bearing zones</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Construction</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Practical testing</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"32":{"title":"High-Stretch Products and Seam Engineering","cat":"Specialized Use","body":["Highly stretchable materials generate substantial recovery forces. Seams may break, pucker, or distort if stitch type, thread, stitch density, feed, needle, and sewing tension are not compatible with the fabric.","Evaluate seam extension, seam growth, recovery, and wearer comfort. At load-bearing areas, consider gussets, seam placement, and load distribution rather than relying only on a higher stitch density.","Test specimens in the actual stretch direction, after the specified care cycle, and under repeated extension. Inspect seams during garment wear as well as on short, static laboratory specimens.","**Technical basis and scope.** High-stretch products often use elastane or knitted structures with substantial extension. Their seams must accommodate body movement and recover after loading. Seam failure may result from thread selection, stitch type, stitch density, seam extensibility, feed settings, needle damage, or cutting that damages elastic fibres.","**Development process and records.** Measure stretch and recovery in the relevant wale/course or warp/weft directions. Establish the extension expected at body zones during movement. Select stitch class, seam construction, thread, needle, and machine settings that can accommodate the required extension. Evaluate seam extension, cyclic stretch, bursting, or wear trials according to the use case; include checks after washing and repeated loading.","**Failure analysis and verification.** If a seam bursts during wear although a static seam test passes, investigate dynamic extension and load distribution. If the fabric tunnels or forms waves, check differential feed, thread tension, needle, and stitch density. Thread tensile strength alone is not a sufficient selection criterion; extensibility and compatibility with the fabric structure must also be considered."],"deep":"Highly stretchable fabric changes spreading, cutting, sewing, and finished-garment measurement. Control the stretch direction, recovery, feed ratio, thread tension, and presser-foot pressure to avoid wavy seams or stretched edges. Measure the garment after the recovery period specified in the process. Test seams under extension in more than one direction rather than pulling only along the body length.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Stretch may come from elastane, knitted loop geometry, mechanical stretch, or a combination of these. The fabric and seam respond differently to extension, recovery, heat, and repeated loading. A seam that is strong but insufficiently extensible can restrict movement or rupture; excessive feed or tension can distort the panel before the garment is worn. Needle damage can also weaken elastane-containing structures.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Measure extension and recovery in all relevant fabric directions and identify the body movements that create the highest local strain. Select stitch and seam classes that provide adequate extensibility without sacrificing seam security. Establish needle type and size, thread, stitch density, differential feed, presser-foot pressure, and sewing speed through trials on production-equivalent material. Record the recovery interval before measurement and define inspection after care and cyclic extension.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** Seam rupture during movement may indicate insufficient seam extension, poor load distribution, or local stress concentration. Wavy seams and tunnelling may result from feed mismatch, excessive tension, or unsuitable presser-foot pressure. Compare the failed garment with the approved sewing settings and material lot. A short static pull test cannot represent every movement cycle or wearing condition.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Develop a seam-engineering plan for a high-stretch garment. Include fabric-direction data, expected body extension, stitch and seam selection, machine settings, recovery time, cyclic test method, and acceptance criteria.","labels":["High-Stretch Products and Seam Engineering","High-Stretch Products and Seam Engineering","Technical control framework for this garment group","Use case","Load-bearing zones","Construction","Practical testing","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"High-Stretch Products and Seam Engineering\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>High-Stretch Products and Seam Engineering</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">High-Stretch Products and Seam Engineering</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use case</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Load-bearing zones</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Construction</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Practical testing</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"33":{"title":"PU/PVC-Coated Fabrics and Synthetic Leather","cat":"Special Materials","body":["Coated materials combine a textile or knitted substrate with a polymer surface layer. Coating adhesion, flexibility, cracking, hydrolysis, heat resistance, and sewability affect the durability of the finished product.","Needles and presser feet may leave marks or pull the coating. Adhesives, solvents, and heat-sealing conditions must be compatible with the material. Seams can become stress-concentration points or potential leakage paths.","Evaluate coating adhesion, flex resistance, hydrolysis where relevant, seam strength, and appearance after storage. Avoid prolonged folded storage in hot and humid conditions when the material is susceptible to damage.","**Technical basis and scope.** PU/PVC-coated fabrics and synthetic leather are multilayer constructions that may include a woven or knitted substrate, polymer coating, and topcoat. These layers can differ in extension, adhesion, softness, hydrolysis resistance, heat tolerance, and chemical compatibility. Cutting and sewing must avoid coating cracks, delamination, and exposed substrate edges.","**Development process and records.** Obtain the supplier's declaration of construction, thickness, mass, backing, polymer type, finish, and care requirements. Evaluate adhesion, flex cracking, hydrolysis or ageing, abrasion, and colour transfer according to the product specification. Trial needles, presser feet, heat sealing, adhesives, and seam construction on representative material. Define the face side, direction of stretch, and spreading orientation.","**Failure analysis and verification.** If the coating sticks to the presser foot or cracks at a fold, investigate heat, pressure, fold radius, material age, and chemical compatibility. Do not use an unapproved cleaning solvent; confirm compatibility with the supplier to avoid damaging the topcoat."],"deep":"PU/PVC-coated materials vary in thickness, softness, textile backing, coating adhesion, and folding behaviour. Assess cracking, delamination, surface tack, hydrolysis, and changes after storage. Select needles, thread, and sewing methods that minimise needle holes and seam tearing. For synthetic leather, control the face side, cutting direction, and presser-foot marks.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** A coated textile is a composite structure, not a homogeneous fabric. The substrate provides much of the tensile behaviour, while the polymer layer controls surface appearance and may provide water resistance, abrasion resistance, or other functions. The topcoat can be sensitive to heat, friction, solvents, and prolonged contact under pressure. PU and PVC formulations do not have identical ageing behaviour, so the material name alone is not enough to define processing limits.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Obtain complete supplier information, including substrate composition, polymer system, coating layers, thickness, mass, finish, lot identification, and recommended storage conditions. Test adhesion, flex cracking, abrasion, hydrolysis or accelerated ageing where relevant, and colour transfer. Conduct sewing and bonding trials with the actual needle, presser foot, thread, adhesive, and machine settings. Protect the material from surface scratches and uncontrolled heat during cutting, sewing, pressing, and packing.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** Delamination may be related to ageing, moisture, heat, chemical exposure, or inadequate adhesion. Cracking at folds may indicate a tight fold radius, low-temperature flexibility limits, or material deterioration. Marks caused by the presser foot should be traced to pressure, friction, surface finish, and machine setup. Do not assume that one PU/PVC material's processing settings apply to another supplier or formulation.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a material and process approval sheet for a coated-fabric product. Record the layer construction, supplier limits, storage conditions, sewing and bonding trials, relevant durability tests, and criteria for approving a production lot.","labels":["PU/PVC-Coated Fabrics and Synthetic Leather","PU/PVC-Coated Fabrics and Synthetic Leather","Technical control framework for this garment group","Material properties","Material","Processing","Durability","Verification","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"PU/PVC-Coated Fabrics and Synthetic Leather\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>PU/PVC-Coated Fabrics and Synthetic Leather</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">PU/PVC-Coated Fabrics and Synthetic Leather</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Material properties</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Material</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Processing</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Durability</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Verification</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"34":{"title":"Reflective, Luminous, and Technical Decorative Materials","cat":"Special Materials","body":["Reflective, luminous, and decorative films may contain adhesive layers, glass beads, microprisms, or pigments. Optical performance and mechanical durability must be assessed separately.","Excessive heat, uneven pressure, or an incompatible fabric finish can cause peeling, cracking, or loss of performance. Decorations placed across stretch zones must also be checked under extension.","Test adhesion, washing, flexing, stretching, and optical performance against the specification. Retain the validated heat-transfer settings for the exact material code and machine.","**Technical basis and scope.** Reflective, luminous, and technical decorative materials work through different mechanisms. Retroreflective materials return light towards its source; photoluminescent materials emit stored light after being charged; active illuminated components require a power source. These technologies are not interchangeable when making safety or visibility claims.","**Development process and records.** Define whether the purpose is identification, decoration, warning, or improved visibility, then select a material and attachment method accordingly. Control width, orientation, placement, bend radius, coverage, and visibility from relevant viewing angles. Test adhesion, folding, washing, abrasion, and optical performance to the specification; verify compatibility with the base fabric and seam construction.","**Failure analysis and verification.** If reflective tape loses performance after washing, trace heat-transfer settings, chemicals, wash cycles, and care instructions. Do not describe a decorative luminous material as PPE or as retroreflective unless it has been tested using the appropriate method."],"deep":"Retroreflective materials return incident light towards its source, whereas photoluminescent materials store energy and emit light in darkness. These are different functions. Placement must preserve visibility and must not be obscured by pockets, folds, or outer layers. Verify adhesion, flexibility, and optical performance after washing or abrasion using the relevant method.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Retroreflective films and tapes may use glass-bead or microprismatic structures. Photoluminescent materials rely on pigments that absorb and later release energy, while active illuminated components require an electrical supply. Each system has different limitations in viewing angle, charging, brightness, power, durability, and care. The intended function must be stated precisely in the specification.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define the visibility or decorative objective, target use environment, viewing directions, placement zones, and applicable performance requirements. Confirm the base fabric, adhesive, transfer method, application temperature, pressure, dwell time, and cooling conditions. Evaluate visibility and adhesion on the finished garment, including after washing, flexing, stretching, and abrasion where required. Retain machine settings and lot traceability for each approved material.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** Peeling may result from incompatible finishes, insufficient bonding, incorrect temperature, uneven pressure, or repeated flexing. Reduced optical performance after care may indicate surface damage, chemical exposure, or use beyond the validated wash limit. Do not infer safety compliance from appearance or from a decorative material description; use the relevant test evidence for the exact product configuration.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Create an approval checklist for a garment using reflective or luminous components. Identify the intended function, material technology, placement, attachment parameters, optical tests, durability checks, care limits, and wording permitted for the product claim.","labels":["Reflective, Luminous, and Technical Decorative Materials","Reflective, Luminous, and Technical Decorative Materials","Technical control framework for this garment group","Material properties","Material","Processing","Durability","Verification","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Reflective, Luminous, and Technical Decorative Materials\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Reflective, Luminous, and Technical Decorative Materials</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Reflective, Luminous, and Technical Decorative Materials</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Material properties</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Material</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Processing</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Durability</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Verification</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"35":{"title":"Soft Bags, Backpacks, and Sewn Accessories","cat":"Specialized Use","body":["Soft bags and backpacks combine multiple materials, webbing, foam, zippers, buckles, and load-bearing seams. Design must define the working load, weight distribution, anchor points, and intended handling.","Bartacks, box-X stitching, binding, and seam allowances must match the applied load and material thickness. Strap-to-body connections are critical risk areas; testing the base fabric alone is insufficient.","Test static and dynamic loads, strap pull, zipper cycles, seam strength, and abrasion as required. Record the test load, duration, suspension method, and failure criteria.","**Technical basis and scope.** Soft bags and backpacks are structures subject to repeated loading. They commonly contain multiple layers, foam, webbing, zippers, buckles, and attachment points. Loads are not distributed evenly; forces concentrate at straps, bases, corners, seams, and hardware anchors. Design requirements must reflect carried mass, lifting method, and use frequency.","**Development process and records.** Define the rated load, expected use factor, container dimensions, and user handling actions. Develop patterns with suitable seam allowances and reinforcement at anchor points; select webbing, buckles, zippers, foam, and lining for their functions. Evaluate seam strength, strap pull, handle strength, zipper cycling, abrasion, and load performance on the complete configuration.","**Failure analysis and verification.** If a strap detaches, trace the load path through the bartack, reinforcement, and base fabric; simply adding stitches may cause the substrate to tear. If a zipper jams under load, examine body distortion, panel curvature, and zipper orientation."],"deep":"Soft bags and backpacks transfer loads through straps, anchor points, bottom seams, closures, and reinforcement panels. Trace the load path from the carried item through the shoulder or handle system and into the bag body. Define the test load, suspension method, cycle count, and use conditions from the product requirements. Inspect fabric tearing, seam slippage, strap deformation, and hardware failure.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Bag performance depends on the interaction of shell fabric, lining, foam, webbing, thread, reinforcement, zippers, buckles, and hardware. Load concentration is often highest where straps connect to the body, at corners, and along the base. Repeated lifting and movement can cause fatigue even when a single static load is passed. Internal dimensions and compartment layout can also change how the load is distributed.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Establish rated load, intended contents, carrying method, expected cycle frequency, and environmental exposure. Specify webbing width and strength, buckle capacity, zipper size and construction, reinforcement dimensions, stitch pattern, thread, seam allowance, and panel materials. Validate the complete bag using static and repeated-load tests appropriate to its use. Include checks for strap pull-out, seam opening, panel distortion, zipper operation under load, and abrasion at contact points.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** A strap failure may originate in weak substrate fabric, insufficient reinforcement, poor stitch placement, or an unsuitable load path. A stronger bartack can worsen failure if it transfers force into a small area of weak fabric. Zipper problems under load may be caused by body distortion or incorrect placement. A fabric tensile result cannot establish the strength of the assembled bag.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Develop a load-validation plan for a soft bag or backpack. Specify the rated load, load distribution, strap and anchor construction, test fixture, static and cyclic tests, inspection points, and acceptance criteria.","labels":["Soft Bags, Backpacks, and Sewn Accessories","Soft Bags, Backpacks, and Sewn Accessories","Technical control framework for this garment group","Use case","Load-bearing zones","Construction","Practical testing","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Soft Bags, Backpacks, and Sewn Accessories\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Soft Bags, Backpacks, and Sewn Accessories</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Soft Bags, Backpacks, and Sewn Accessories</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use case</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Load-bearing zones</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Construction</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Practical testing</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"36":{"title":"Integrating Materials, Patterns, and Processes for Specialized Products","cat":"Integrated Engineering","body":["Functional materials may differ from conventional fabrics in shrinkage, thickness, friction, and heat limits. Patterns, seam allowances, needles, thread, guides, and sewing sequence must be developed together.","A material substitution after approval can change fit, seam-tape bonding, adhesive performance, needle penetration, and test results. Materials with the same fibre content must not automatically be treated as equivalent.","Conduct a design-for-manufacturing review and pilot using bulk-equivalent materials. Retain the approved reference sample, machine settings, bill of materials, test reports, and defect-handling process.","**Technical basis and scope.** Specialized products require coordinated material, pattern, and process development because a change in any one layer can affect the whole system. Greater material thickness changes turn of cloth; different stretch behaviour changes fit; coatings may restrict needle, presser-foot, and heat-sealing choices. The specification must therefore describe the exact configuration that has been verified.","**Development process and records.** Establish a configuration matrix covering fabric code, finish, lining, insulation, thread, closure, pattern revision, seam type, machine settings, and test status. When a component changes, conduct an impact assessment covering fit, function, sewability, durability, care, and claims. Run a pilot on the intended equipment and factory before bulk release.","**Failure analysis and verification.** If an approved sample performs well but bulk production fails, compare material lots, pattern revisions, machines, operators, and environmental conditions. Do not accept a supposedly equivalent material until the properties affecting product function have been checked."],"deep":"Materials, patterns, and production processes must be developed as one coordinated system. Changes in thickness or shrinkage can affect patterns, seams, seam sealing, and finished measurements. Prepare a compatibility matrix for materials, needles, thread, presser feet, machines, adhesives, and finishing methods. Validate the assembled sample before release and record the exact configuration tested.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Specialized garments may combine coated shells, membranes, insulation, elastic materials, reinforcement, and technical closures. Each component influences thickness, friction, extension, heat response, and assembly sequence. A material change can alter turn of cloth, fit balance, seam bulk, needle penetration, adhesive bonding, or functional test results. Component-level equivalence does not guarantee garment-level equivalence.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Maintain a configuration matrix linking material codes and lots to pattern revision, bill of materials, seam construction, thread, needle, machine settings, adhesives, and test evidence. Conduct design-for-manufacturing reviews with production and quality teams. Pilot the approved configuration on intended equipment, then evaluate fit, function, sewability, durability, care performance, and relevant claims. Establish formal change control before substituting any component.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** When bulk differs from the approved sample, compare material lot, finish, shrinkage, pattern revision, machine setup, operator method, and factory environment. Identify which change affected the result rather than adjusting several variables at once. A supplier declaration or matching fibre composition is not enough to establish equivalence. Retest the affected functions whenever the impact assessment requires it.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Build a configuration-control matrix for a specialized garment. Include material and component codes, pattern revision, seam and machine parameters, pilot evidence, test status, change triggers, approval owners, and the procedure for evaluating a proposed substitution.","labels":["Integrating Materials, Patterns, and Processes for Specialized Products","Integrating Materials, Patterns, and Processes for Specialized Products","Technical control framework for this garment group","Materials","Pattern","Process","Finished garment","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Integrating Materials, Patterns, and Processes for Specialized Products\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Integrating Materials, Patterns, and Processes for Specialized Products</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Integrating Materials, Patterns, and Processes for Specialized Products</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Materials</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pattern</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Process</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Finished garment</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"37":{"title":"Wear Trials, Field Testing, and Life-Cycle Evaluation","cat":"Integrated Engineering","body":["Wear trials collect feedback under actual use conditions; field tests examine defined activities and environments; laboratory tests measure properties under controlled conditions. These forms of evidence complement one another but are not interchangeable.","Prepare the protocol before testing: participants, duration, activities, criteria, care conditions, recording method, and incident response. PPE field tests must never expose participants to uncontrolled hazards.","Combine defect, repair, return, and material-degradation data to update design risks. A short test cannot establish the full service life of a garment.","**Technical basis and scope.** Wear trials and field tests assess products in real-use conditions, where interactions among the wearer, activity, climate, and care may differ from laboratory conditions. A useful trial requires predefined hypotheses, participant groups, tasks, duration, and observable data.","**Development process and records.** Prepare a protocol covering objectives, participant selection, sizes, duration, activities, environmental conditions, logs, photographs or video, and feedback methods. Record product configuration, lot, care history, and deviations. Analyse quantitative measurements alongside qualitative feedback, separating product defects from individual differences or test conditions.","**Failure analysis and verification.** A single comment does not establish a widespread defect. If a safety incident, irritation, or functional failure occurs, apply the stop-test and reporting procedure. Do not generalise findings from a small trial group to an entire population or every environment."],"deep":"Wear trials evaluate user experience during actual activities; field tests add defined environmental conditions and tasks. Specify participants, duration, movements, washing cycles, and recording methods. Track defects, comfort, deformation, and loss of function. Life-cycle evaluation should also consider repairability, replacement of components, cleaning, and end-of-life handling.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Real-use performance is influenced by wearer morphology, garment size, movement, climate, work intensity, care practices, and interactions with other equipment. Laboratory testing isolates selected properties under controlled conditions, while wear trials reveal how the complete product behaves in context. Neither evidence type should be used as a substitute for the other when the requirement calls for both.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define the trial objective, participant profile, size range, activities, duration, environmental conditions, care cycles, observation points, and data-collection method before distributing samples. Record the exact garment configuration and lot. Use structured rating scales and objective measurements where possible, and provide a clear route for reporting discomfort, damage, or safety incidents. For PPE, define prohibited exposures and stop-test conditions in advance. Include repair, component replacement, cleaning, and disposal considerations in the life-cycle review.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** Separate recurring product issues from isolated fit preferences, incorrect use, and unusual environmental conditions. Investigate complaints alongside inspection records, photographs, repair data, and laboratory results. Stop the trial when a safety concern or loss of protective function is identified. Small participant groups and short observation periods cannot establish universal suitability or complete service life.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Draft a wear-trial and field-test protocol for a specialized garment. Include participant criteria, activities, duration, environment, data fields, incident controls, care cycles, repair observations, and limits on how results may be generalised.","labels":["Wear Trials, Field Testing, and Life-Cycle Evaluation","Wear Trials, Field Testing, and Life-Cycle Evaluation","Technical control framework for this garment group","Materials","Pattern","Process","Finished garment","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Wear Trials, Field Testing, and Life-Cycle Evaluation\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Wear Trials, Field Testing, and Life-Cycle Evaluation</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Wear Trials, Field Testing, and Life-Cycle Evaluation</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Materials</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pattern</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Process</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Finished garment</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"38":{"title":"Care Instructions and Maintenance Limitations","cat":"Integrated Engineering","body":["Care labels must reflect the washing, drying, ironing, bleaching, and dry-cleaning limits of the complete garment, not only the shell fabric. Adhesives, membranes, reflective trims, elastane, insulation, and accessories may be the limiting components.","Test the intended care process on the finished garment, then assess colour, dimensions, function, seams, coatings, and components. Where specialist care is required, the instructions must be clear and practical.","Do not assign care symbols by assumption. Check the target market's labelling rules and retain the test evidence supporting the instructions.","**Technical basis and scope.** Care instructions form part of the product configuration because washing, drying, ironing, bleaching, and dry cleaning can change dimensions, shade, finish, adhesion, function, and service life. For technical products, incorrect care may remove a function even when the original material passed its performance test.","**Development process and records.** Define the intended care process and market requirements, then test the finished garment using the specified method. Monitor shrinkage, skewing, colour loss, pilling, seams, coatings, reflective elements, membranes, elastics, and accessories. Write instructions from test evidence and supplier confirmation; state restrictions such as no bleach or no tumble drying only when supported.","**Failure analysis and verification.** If a customer uses a care process different from the label, distinguish product failure from misuse using test reports, labels, and retained samples. Do not assign care symbols by guesswork; verify the applicable market labelling rules."],"deep":"Care instructions must be based on the actual product construction, including the base material, coating, seam tape, elastic, reflective elements, and accessories. Evaluate the garment after the relevant washing or disinfection cycles. Specify temperature, chemicals, drying method, and necessary restrictions based on verified results. General advice for one fabric type may not suit a multilayer garment.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** The garment's care tolerance is determined by its most sensitive components and interfaces. Heat may affect elastane, adhesives, coatings, prints, membranes, and foam; chemicals may alter dyes, finishes, or reflective surfaces; mechanical action may damage seams, trims, and insulation. The shell fabric alone cannot define the care procedure for a composite product.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Identify the target market and applicable care-labelling rules. Test the complete garment using the intended washing, drying, ironing, bleaching, or dry-cleaning process, including the required number of cycles. Record dimensional change, colourfastness, appearance, seam integrity, adhesion, waterproofness or other functional properties, and accessory performance. Confirm supplier restrictions and retain the test reports linked to the approved product revision. Use only symbols and wording permitted by the market rules.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** If a product loses function after care, compare the actual process with the validated instructions and examine the most sensitive components. Determine whether the issue is caused by an unsuitable label, a manufacturing variation, or care outside the validated conditions. Do not assume that a fabric-level care result covers seam tape, insulation, prints, or hardware.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a care-validation record for a multilayer or functional garment. List each component, its care limits, the complete-garment test cycles, measured changes, permitted label symbols, and the evidence required for approval.","labels":["Care Instructions and Maintenance Limitations","Care Instructions and Maintenance Limitations","Technical control framework for this garment group","Materials","Pattern","Process","Finished garment","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Care Instructions and Maintenance Limitations\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Care Instructions and Maintenance Limitations</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Care Instructions and Maintenance Limitations</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Materials</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pattern</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Process</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Finished garment</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"39":{"title":"Packaging, Transport, and Storage of Technical Products","cat":"Integrated Engineering","body":["Products containing coatings, membranes, foam, insulation, or heat-bonded components may be damaged by compression, heat, moisture, and storage duration. Packaging must protect the product without causing condensation or trapped odour.","Define folding method, hangers, bags, approved desiccants where needed, stacking limits, and warehouse conditions. Do not seal products in bags while they are still damp or contain residual chemicals.","Use risk-based transport or storage simulations covering compression, vibration, temperature, humidity, and duration. Inspect the product after unpacking, not only the carton.","**Technical basis and scope.** Packaging and storage can affect shape, moisture, colour, coatings, foam, elastomers, and adhesive materials. Prolonged compression may reduce loft or create permanent creases; high temperature and humidity may accelerate hydrolysis, surface blocking, or corrosion of hardware.","**Development process and records.** Define transport conditions, storage duration, folding or rolling method, protective bags, desiccants where necessary, and stacking limits. Conduct packaging simulations using the actual configuration; inspect distortion, odour, moisture, colour transfer, and function after unpacking. Record lot, packing date, warehouse conditions, and FIFO or FEFO rules where appropriate to the material.","**Failure analysis and verification.** If a product arrives flattened or delaminated, investigate compression duration, container temperature, humidity, packaging material, and stacking load. One transport simulation must not be assumed to represent every route or seasonal climate."],"deep":"Packaging and storage can affect coatings, elastics, foam, bonded layers, and garment shape. Define folding, compression, humidity, temperature, storage duration, and stacking conditions. Use compatible packaging and prevent colour transfer or surface blocking. Protect traceability labels and user instructions so that they remain legible at delivery.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Technical garments may contain membranes, coatings, foam, insulation, elastomers, adhesives, and hardware that respond differently to compression, temperature, moisture, and contact. Long-term compression can reduce loft or create creases; humidity may contribute to hydrolysis or corrosion; contact between incompatible surfaces may cause transfer or blocking. Packaging is therefore part of product preservation, not merely a shipping operation.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Define the expected transport route, transit duration, warehouse conditions, folding or hanging method, bag material, ventilation, desiccant use where justified, and stacking limits. Validate the actual packed configuration through risk-based compression, vibration, temperature, and humidity simulations. Inspect the unpacked garment for shape recovery, surface condition, odour, moisture, colour transfer, component function, and label legibility. Record lot, packing date, storage limits, and stock-rotation rules appropriate to the material.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** Flattened insulation may result from excessive compression or storage time; delamination may relate to heat, humidity, ageing, or incompatible packaging contact. Investigate actual route conditions and compare them with the simulation. Avoid sealing damp garments or products with residual process chemicals. A carton passing a transport test does not prove that the product inside remains functional.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Create a packaging and storage specification for a technical garment. Define folding or hanging, protective packaging, environmental limits, stacking rules, simulation tests, post-unpacking inspections, traceability, and release criteria.","labels":["Packaging, Transport, and Storage of Technical Products","Packaging, Transport, and Storage of Technical Products","Technical control framework for this garment group","Materials","Pattern","Process","Finished garment","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Packaging, Transport, and Storage of Technical Products\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Packaging, Transport, and Storage of Technical Products</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Packaging, Transport, and Storage of Technical Products</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Materials</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pattern</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Process</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Finished garment</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"40":{"title":"Developing a Test Matrix for Specialized Products","cat":"Quality Control","body":["A test matrix links claims, hazards, materials, construction, test methods, acceptance criteria, production stages, and approval owners. A rain jacket, for example, may require hydrostatic-head, seam-leakage, zipper, spray, washing, and wear-trial evaluations.","Specify whether each test applies to fabric, a component, or the finished garment. A fabric test does not prove that seams or zippers perform adequately; a garment test must identify the tested area and configuration.","Control test-matrix revisions by market and buyer. When material, supplier, construction, or process changes, conduct an impact assessment to determine which tests must be repeated.","**Technical basis and scope.** A test matrix for specialized products must trace each requirement and risk to a test method, specimen configuration, test conditions, acceptance criteria, and decision. Not every product requires every test; selection depends on the use case, claims, target market, defect history, and material changes.","**Development process and records.** Build a requirement–hazard–test–sample–acceptance–owner matrix. Distinguish raw-material, component, seam, and finished-garment testing. Define whether specimens are tested as new, after washing, after ageing, or after simulated use. Record the standard and edition, conditioning, sample count, sampling location, equipment, laboratory, and treatment of nonconforming results.","**Failure analysis and verification.** A test report that does not identify the product configuration or revision must not be used to approve bulk production. When colour, finish, supplier, seam, or accessory changes, reassess which tests need repetition. Do not select convenient indicators while ignoring the principal product risks."],"deep":"A test matrix connects requirements and claims to risks, test methods, representative specimens, acceptance criteria, laboratories, and test timing. It should cover materials, finished construction, components, care, and use conditions. Identify which tests are required before the pilot and which must be confirmed on bulk production. Control samples, revisions, and reports so that evidence is never assigned to the wrong configuration.\n\n**Technical characteristics of the product group.** Specialized products may carry performance claims related to water resistance, flame resistance, visibility, chemical protection, stretch, durability, or hygiene. Each claim has a defined scope and evidence requirement. Testing a fabric coupon cannot establish the performance of a complete garment where seams, closures, interfaces, fit, and coverage affect the outcome. Test selection must follow the product's actual hazards and intended use.\n\n**Development and production controls.** Build a traceable matrix linking each requirement to its hazard, material or component, test method, specimen level, test condition, acceptance limit, responsible owner, and approval stage. Identify standards and editions, conditioning, sample size, sampling locations, equipment, laboratory, and retest triggers. Separate raw-material tests from component, seam, and finished-garment tests. Define whether validation is required on new specimens, after care, after ageing, or following simulated use. Keep reports linked to the exact material lots, pattern revision, and product configuration.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification limits.** If a report omits the tested revision or specimen details, its relevance to bulk approval is uncertain. When a supplier, colour, finish, seam, accessory, or process changes, perform an impact assessment before deciding that previous results remain valid. Investigate failed results rather than selectively excluding unfavourable indicators. A passed test supports only the scope and conditions actually evaluated.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Create a test matrix for one specialized garment. Include each claim and hazard, the required specimen level, test method and edition, sample plan, acceptance criteria, timing, owner, report reference, and change triggers for retesting.","labels":["Developing a Test Matrix for Specialized Products","Developing a Test Matrix for Specialized Products","Technical control framework for this garment group","Claim / requirement","Test matrix","Evidence","Decision","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Developing a Test Matrix for Specialized Products\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Developing a Test Matrix for Specialized Products</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Developing a Test Matrix for Specialized Products</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical control framework for this garment group</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Claim / requirement</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Test matrix</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Evidence</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"41":{"title":"Managing Technical Claims and Product Communications","cat":"Quality Control","body":["Claims such as waterproof, breathable, anti-odor, UV protective, flame resistant, antimicrobial, or recycled content require evidence appropriate to the specific claim. A technology name or raw-material certificate does not, by itself, prove finished-product performance.","Define the exact claim, its scope, conditions, functional lifetime, test method, and limitations. Avoid absolute wording such as “completely waterproof” when testing covers only a limited set of conditions.","Marketing, design, QA, and compliance should review claim wording before release. Retain test reports, supply-chain documentation, labels, and the approved version of advertising content.","**Technical basis and scope.** A technical claim must describe the function, scope, conditions, and limitations precisely. Terms such as waterproof, breathable, antimicrobial, UV protective, flame resistant, and recycled may be subject to different evidence and regulatory requirements in different markets. A claim supported for a raw material does not automatically apply to the finished product.","**Implementation process and records.** Create a claim register containing the proposed wording, market, SKU, intended user, applicable standard or method, supporting report, expiry date or review trigger, and approver. Check that labels, websites, catalogues, and sales materials are consistent with the evidence. Retain chain-of-custody records or test reports that match the exact claim scope.","**Failure analysis and verification.** If a claim cannot be substantiated, revise or remove it before release. Do not detach a test result from its test conditions to imply broader performance, and do not use a supplier certificate to support a product with a different configuration."],"deep":"A claim must be specific and supported by evidence that matches its scope and conditions. Avoid absolute wording such as “completely waterproof” when the test method evaluates only a limited condition. Link marketing statements to test reports, the verified material configuration, care instructions, and the period for which the evidence remains valid. Reassess claims when a supplier or construction changes.\n\n**Technical basis and scope.** A technical claim must describe the function, scope, conditions, and limitations precisely. Terms such as waterproof, breathable, antimicrobial, UV protective, flame resistant, and recycled may be subject to different evidence and regulatory requirements in different markets. A claim supported for a raw material does not automatically apply to the finished product.\n\n**Implementation process and records.** Create a claim register containing the proposed wording, market, SKU, intended user, applicable standard or method, supporting report, expiry date or review trigger, and approver. Check that labels, websites, catalogues, and sales materials are consistent with the evidence. Retain chain-of-custody records or test reports that match the exact claim scope.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification.** If a claim cannot be substantiated, revise or remove it before release. Do not detach a test result from its test conditions to imply broader performance, and do not use a supplier certificate to support a product with a different configuration.\n\n**Applied exercise:** For the topic “Managing Technical Claims and Product Communications,” prepare an implementation dossier for a specific style or process. State the input requirements, evaluated configuration, baseline data, responsible owner, verification method, decision criteria, and conditions that require a stop or renewed approval. Use a hypothetical failure to demonstrate root-cause tracing and revision of the control documentation.","labels":["Managing Technical Claims and Product Communications","Managing Technical Claims and Product Communications","Technical claim control and evidence","Claim / requirement","Test matrix","Evidence","Decision","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Managing Technical Claims and Product Communications\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Managing Technical Claims and Product Communications</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Managing Technical Claims and Product Communications</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Technical claim control and evidence</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Claim / requirement</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Test matrix</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Evidence</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"42":{"title":"Evaluating Suppliers of Specialized Materials","cat":"Supply Chain","body":["Suppliers should be assessed for technical capability, lot consistency, documentation, lead time, minimum order quantity, defect-response support, and their ability to maintain material availability throughout the product life cycle.","Request approved reference samples, TDS/SDS, test reports, lot traceability, change notifications, and a nonconformity-handling procedure. For protective materials, verify the scope of certification and the traceability supporting it.","Use trial results and delivery and quality data in the evaluation; do not select a supplier on price alone. A change of source requires a plan to revalidate both the material and the garment.","**Technical basis and scope.** Suppliers of specialized materials should be evaluated for technical capability, quality consistency, traceability, chemical compliance, testing capability, and continuity of supply. A quoted price and a sales swatch are insufficient to establish the suitability of a functional material.","**Implementation process and records.** Use a supplier qualification checklist covering the quality system, raw-material sources, lot controls, change notification, testing capability, capacity, lead time, corrective action, and market documentation. Run a trial lot, review the COA or test report, and compare it with the approved reference sample. For critical materials, require advance notification of changes to formulation, finish, factory, or accessory sources.","**Failure analysis and verification.** When a bulk lot differs from the approved sample, trace the batch, recipe, process window, and measurement method. Do not accept documentation that cannot be linked to the material code, lot, or claim scope. Reassess the supplier after a serious failure or process change."],"deep":"Supplier evaluation should go beyond price and swatch approval. Review lot control, traceability, raw-material changes, colour consistency, documentation, and lead-time capability. For specialized materials, examine test data, defect-handling procedures, and the supplier’s ability to provide reference samples. Agree on advance notification before any material change.\n\n**Technical basis and scope.** Suppliers of specialized materials should be evaluated for technical capability, quality consistency, traceability, chemical compliance, testing capability, and continuity of supply. A quoted price and a sales swatch are insufficient to establish the suitability of a functional material.\n\n**Implementation process and records.** Use a supplier qualification checklist covering the quality system, raw-material sources, lot controls, change notification, testing capability, capacity, lead time, corrective action, and market documentation. Run a trial lot, review the COA or test report, and compare it with the approved reference sample. For critical materials, require advance notification of changes to formulation, finish, factory, or accessory sources.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification.** When a bulk lot differs from the approved sample, trace the batch, recipe, process window, and measurement method. Do not accept documentation that cannot be linked to the material code, lot, or claim scope. Reassess the supplier after a serious failure or process change.\n\n**Applied exercise:** For the topic “Evaluating Suppliers of Specialized Materials,” prepare an implementation dossier for a specific style or process. State the input requirements, evaluated configuration, baseline data, responsible owner, verification method, decision criteria, and conditions that require a stop or renewed approval. Use a hypothetical failure to demonstrate root-cause tracing and revision of the control documentation.","labels":["Evaluating Suppliers of Specialized Materials","Evaluating Suppliers of Specialized Materials","Supplier qualification and material control","Supplier","Sample and lot","Pilot and traceability","Release","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Evaluating Suppliers of Specialized Materials\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Evaluating Suppliers of Specialized Materials</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Evaluating Suppliers of Specialized Materials</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Supplier qualification and material control</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Supplier</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Sample and lot</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pilot and traceability</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Release</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"43":{"title":"Pilot Runs, Scale-Up, and Production Approval for Specialized Garments","cat":"Supply Chain","body":["A pilot should simulate bulk conditions: machines, operators, materials, layout, and feeding rhythm. Processes such as seam sealing, denim washing, bonding, and sewing multiple layers require confirmation of equipment capability.","Scale-up can change heat transfer, pressing force, chemical loading, speed, tension, and defect rates. Results from a small laboratory sample do not guarantee stable performance at production volume.","Release bulk production only after the control plan, work instructions, test matrix, approved sample, machine capability, and defect-handling process have been agreed. Apply enhanced monitoring to the initial lots.","**Technical basis and scope.** Pilot production and scale-up assess repeatability when development samples are transferred to the equipment, workforce, and output levels planned for production. A sample-room result does not establish bulk-process stability, particularly for multilayer materials, special operations, or processes requiring environmental control.","**Implementation process and records.** Define the pilot objective, quantity, machines, operators, materials, pattern revision, and evaluation criteria. Track first-pass yield, defect modes, cycle time, rework, material loss, and test results. Record every deviation, classify its impact, close corrective and preventive actions, and verify the process again after adjustment. Bulk release requires documented approval of all critical points.","**Failure analysis and verification.** If the pilot passes but defects appear at the start of bulk production, investigate differences in equipment, operator skill, material lots, layout, and speed. Do not increase output to recover the schedule before the cause of recurring defects is understood."],"deep":"Scale-up must confirm that the pilot process can be repeated with the equipment, workforce, and production rate planned for bulk. Monitor material variation, machine parameters, quality, repair rates, and bottlenecks. The pilot sample must represent the approved configuration. Any deviation from the reference sample requires assessment and approval before bulk production.\n\n**Technical basis and scope.** Pilot production and scale-up assess repeatability when development samples are transferred to the equipment, workforce, and output levels planned for production. A sample-room result does not establish bulk-process stability, particularly for multilayer materials, special operations, or processes requiring environmental control.\n\n**Implementation process and records.** Define the pilot objective, quantity, machines, operators, materials, pattern revision, and evaluation criteria. Track first-pass yield, defect modes, cycle time, rework, material loss, and test results. Record every deviation, classify its impact, close corrective and preventive actions, and verify the process again after adjustment. Bulk release requires documented approval of all critical points.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification.** If the pilot passes but defects appear at the start of bulk production, investigate differences in equipment, operator skill, material lots, layout, and speed. Do not increase output to recover the schedule before the cause of recurring defects is understood.\n\n**Applied exercise:** For the topic “Pilot Runs, Scale-Up, and Production Approval for Specialized Garments,” prepare an implementation dossier for a specific style or process. State the input requirements, evaluated configuration, baseline data, responsible owner, verification method, decision criteria, and conditions that require a stop or renewed approval. Use a hypothetical failure to demonstrate root-cause tracing and revision of the control documentation.","labels":["Pilot Runs, Scale-Up, and Production Approval for Specialized Garments","Pilot Runs, Scale-Up, and Production Approval for Specialized Garments","Pilot validation and production release","Supplier","Sample and lot","Pilot and traceability","Release","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Pilot Runs, Scale-Up, and Production Approval for Specialized Garments\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Pilot Runs, Scale-Up, and Production Approval for Specialized Garments</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pilot Runs, Scale-Up, and Production Approval for Specialized Garments</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Pilot validation and production release</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Supplier</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Sample and lot</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pilot and traceability</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Release</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"44":{"title":"Investigating Specialized-Product Failures and CAPA","cat":"Supply Chain","body":["Failures in specialized products often arise from interactions among materials, patterns, components, processes, users, and care. Investigation should reproduce the conditions under which the failure occurred rather than examine only one component.","Classify failure modes such as seam leakage, membrane delamination, loss of elasticity, coating cracks, reinforcement pull-out, reduced reflectivity, uneven insulation fill, or fit failure during movement. Collect failed and conforming samples together with lot data.","Use 5 Whys, fishbone analysis, and controlled trials to establish the cause. CAPA must demonstrate effectiveness on subsequent lots and update the design or test matrix when necessary.","**Technical basis and scope.** Investigation of a specialized-product failure should begin with an observable description and traceability data, not an assumption about who caused it. The same symptom may originate in materials, design, processing, care, or conditions of use.","**Implementation process and records.** Contain and scope the issue by SKU, lot, shift, machine, supplier, and test condition. Use photographs, failed samples, defect maps, 5 Whys, or fishbone analysis to test hypotheses. Define containment before detailed investigation. CAPA should include a verified root cause, action, owner, due date, effectiveness criteria, and recurrence checks.","**Failure analysis and verification.** Do not close CAPA merely because operators have been retrained. If the cause is a fixture design or an unclear specification, the action must correct the system. Monitor subsequent lots and verify that the failure does not recur under equivalent conditions."],"deep":"Investigate specialized-product failures by narrowing the scope to material lots, machines, operations, operators, washing conditions, and product configuration. Separate the observed symptom from assumed causes; use failed samples, photographs, measurements, and controlled reproduction. CAPA must address the systemic cause, assign an owner, and be checked for effectiveness on the next lot.\n\n**Technical basis and scope.** Investigation of a specialized-product failure should begin with an observable description and traceability data, not an assumption about who caused it. The same symptom may originate in materials, design, processing, care, or conditions of use.\n\n**Implementation process and records.** Contain and scope the issue by SKU, lot, shift, machine, supplier, and test condition. Use photographs, failed samples, defect maps, 5 Whys, or fishbone analysis to test hypotheses. Define containment before detailed investigation. CAPA should include a verified root cause, action, owner, due date, effectiveness criteria, and recurrence checks.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification.** Do not close CAPA merely because operators have been retrained. If the cause is a fixture design or an unclear specification, the action must correct the system. Monitor subsequent lots and verify that the failure does not recur under equivalent conditions.\n\n**Applied exercise:** For the topic “Investigating Specialized-Product Failures and CAPA,” prepare an implementation dossier for a specific style or process. State the input requirements, evaluated configuration, baseline data, responsible owner, verification method, decision criteria, and conditions that require a stop or renewed approval. Use a hypothetical failure to demonstrate root-cause tracing and revision of the control documentation.","labels":["Investigating Specialized-Product Failures and CAPA","Investigating Specialized-Product Failures and CAPA","Failure investigation and corrective action","Supplier","Sample and lot","Pilot and traceability","Release","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Investigating Specialized-Product Failures and CAPA\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Investigating Specialized-Product Failures and CAPA</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Investigating Specialized-Product Failures and CAPA</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Failure investigation and corrective action</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Supplier</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Sample and lot</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pilot and traceability</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Release</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"45":{"title":"Technical Documentation and Traceability for Specialized Products","cat":"Supply Chain","body":["A style dossier should retain the brief, risk assessment, pattern, tech pack, BOM, approved materials, test matrix, reports, PPS, pilot record, care label, and bulk-release approval. All records should be linked to the style code and revision.","For products carrying protective claims, add the certification scope, use instructions, maintenance requirements, periodic inspection, and retirement conditions. Records must allow finished-product lots to be traced back to material lots.","When a complaint or regulatory change occurs, rapid traceability helps identify affected styles, purchase orders, lots, markets, and customers. Approval records should not exist only in an individual’s email account.","**Technical basis and scope.** Technical documentation and traceability for specialized products must connect requirements, configuration, materials, suppliers, revisions, testing, production, and care. A missing link makes it difficult to identify affected lots after a change or complaint.","**Implementation process and records.** Establish a product dossier containing the approved specification, pattern and tech pack, BOM, material certificates, test reports, risk assessment, pilot approval, batch records, label artwork, care instructions, and change log. Use unique identifiers for styles, components, lots, and revisions; define editing rights and retention periods according to contractual and legal requirements.","**Failure analysis and verification.** When a complaint occurs, the organization must be able to trace the configuration and component lots used. A folder of disconnected files is not a traceability system unless it includes code relationships, approval status, and change history."],"deep":"Documentation should link the style code, material lots, suppliers, tech-pack revision, test results, approved sample, and change history. Traceability should work in both directions: from a finished product back to its raw materials, and from a material lot forward to every order in which it was used. Record-retention rules should make relevant samples and reports easy to retrieve during a complaint or recall.\n\n**Technical basis and scope.** Technical documentation and traceability for specialized products must connect requirements, configuration, materials, suppliers, revisions, testing, production, and care. A missing link makes it difficult to identify affected lots after a change or complaint.\n\n**Implementation process and records.** Establish a product dossier containing the approved specification, pattern and tech pack, BOM, material certificates, test reports, risk assessment, pilot approval, batch records, label artwork, care instructions, and change log. Use unique identifiers for styles, components, lots, and revisions; define editing rights and retention periods according to contractual and legal requirements.\n\n**Failure analysis and verification.** When a complaint occurs, the organization must be able to trace the configuration and component lots used. A folder of disconnected files is not a traceability system unless it includes code relationships, approval status, and change history.\n\n**Applied exercise:** For the topic “Technical Documentation and Traceability for Specialized Products,” prepare an implementation dossier for a specific style or process. State the input requirements, evaluated configuration, baseline data, responsible owner, verification method, decision criteria, and conditions that require a stop or renewed approval. Use a hypothetical failure to demonstrate root-cause tracing and revision of the control documentation.","labels":["Technical Documentation and Traceability for Specialized Products","Technical Documentation and Traceability for Specialized Products","Product dossier and traceability control","Supplier","Sample and lot","Pilot and traceability","Release","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Technical Documentation and Traceability for Specialized Products\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Technical Documentation and Traceability for Specialized Products</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Technical Documentation and Traceability for Specialized Products</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Product dossier and traceability control</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Supplier</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Sample and lot</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Pilot and traceability</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Release</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"46":{"title":"Case Study: Developing a Rain Jacket from Use Case to Pilot","cat":"Advanced Practice","body":["Illustrative case: a brand needs a motorcycle jacket for urban commuting in moderate rain, worn with an external backpack and frequent mounting and dismounting. The initial brief says “waterproof, breathable, lightweight.” Before selecting fabric, the development team must clarify rain exposure duration, wearer posture, flex zones, closure use, backpack position, and care conditions.","Map potential water-entry paths: face fabric, shoulder seams, underarm, sleeve seams, zipper, pockets, collar, cuffs, and hem. For each location, specify the intended construction, such as a membrane or coating, seam tape, storm flap, zipper garage, or cuff adjustment. Do not assume that a water-resistant fabric makes the complete jacket waterproof.","Divide validation into three levels: material tests, detail tests for zippers, seams, and components, and complete-garment tests and wear trials. Record the method, sample revision, test conditions, and criteria approved by the buyer or technical team. Spray testing, hydrostatic-head testing, and seam-leakage testing answer different questions.","Before the pilot, freeze the BOM and pattern revision. Check seam-tape bonding on the actual fabric layers, zipper compatibility, seam placement, and operator method. During the pilot, sample at the beginning, middle, and end of the shift; record tape lifting, puckering, needle holes, zipper jamming, and leakage at seams. Release only after open issues have corrective actions and verification records.","Practical form: use case / risk zone / construction / test / approved criterion / result / approver / revision. Requirements and results in this case must be established for the specific product, market, and buyer; they are not universal technical specifications."],"deep":"A rain-jacket case begins by defining rainfall, wind, exposure duration, activity, and required coverage. Select the face fabric, coating or membrane, lining, seam tape, and storm-flap or zipper construction. Build samples to assess movement, water-entry paths, and wearer comfort. The pilot confirms seam-taping operations, dimensional stability, and consistency across units.","labels":["Case Study: Developing a Rain Jacket from Use Case to Pilot","Case Study: Developing a Rain Jacket from Use Case to Pilot","Rain-jacket development and pilot validation","Use case","Risk analysis","Testing","Decision","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Developing a Rain Jacket from Use Case to Pilot\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Developing a Rain Jacket from Use Case to Pilot</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Case Study: Developing a Rain Jacket from Use Case to Pilot</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Rain-jacket development and pilot validation</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use case</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Risk analysis</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Testing</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"47":{"title":"Case Study: Designing and Controlling PPE Against Defined Hazards","cat":"Advanced Practice","body":["Illustrative case: a factory is asked to develop garments for maintenance staff who may work under several exposure conditions. A brief that says only “PPE fabric” is insufficient. The team must ask the safety lead to define each hazard, exposure level, activity, wearing duration, environment, base layers, and equipment used with the garment.","Do not combine different hazards into one general claim. Chemical protection, antistatic performance, heat protection, arc protection, and cut resistance use different protection mechanisms, constructions, and test methods. If the product carries multiple claims, create a separate matrix for each hazard and check compatibility among the requirements.","An example matrix includes: hazard; body area to protect; applicable standard and edition; materials and components; seam and closure construction; material- or garment-level tests; approved criteria; documentation; use limitations; and maintenance instructions. Clearly flag evidence gaps and do not turn unverified functions into marketing claims.","Conduct a design review with QA, engineering, compliance, suppliers, and user representatives. Review gaps at cuffs and hems, coverage when bending or reaching, compatibility with gloves, footwear, and helmets, and the risk of component degradation after washing. Do not conduct field trials that expose wearers to uncontrolled hazards.","Required outputs include a risk assessment, test plan, certificates within their stated scope, labels and instructions, and a change-control process. This training material does not replace workplace risk assessment, specialist advice, or the legal requirements of the target market."],"deep":"A PPE case requires hazard analysis before material or garment-shape selection. Define protected body zones, exposure type, duration, conditions of use, and applicable standards. Design closures, seams, overlaps, and interfaces with other equipment. Build a test matrix, test representative samples, and document use limitations and maintenance conditions before making a release decision.","labels":["Case Study: Designing and Controlling PPE Against Defined Hazards","Case Study: Designing and Controlling PPE Against Defined Hazards","PPE hazard assessment and release control","Hazard","Protection design","Testing","Release decision","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Designing and Controlling PPE Against Defined Hazards\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Designing and Controlling PPE Against Defined Hazards</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Case Study: Designing and Controlling PPE Against Defined Hazards</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">PPE hazard assessment and release control</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Hazard</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Protection design</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Testing</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Release decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"48":{"title":"Case Study: Reviewing Childrenswear Safety Before Release","cat":"Advanced Practice","body":["Illustrative case: a hooded children's jacket includes adjustment cords, a zipper, snaps, and decorative labels. Before sample approval, identify the exact age group, sales market, intended use, and current applicable requirements. One checklist must not be assumed suitable for every age group or country.","Review every detail that may create a hazard: drawstrings and loops; buttons, beads, charms, or detachable trims; zipper ends and sharp components; straps; decorative labels; seams that may open; and materials or chemicals subject to control. Check the garment when new, after washing, and after relevant use-related stresses.","Create a design-control table containing the component, hazard, age group, legal or standard requirement to consult, assessment method, test sample, result, action, and approver. Tests such as trim pull, small-parts assessment, drawstring checks, and chemical testing must follow the official methods and limits for the target market.","During fitting, observe dressing and undressing, visibility when the hood is worn, zipper operation, and areas that could catch on objects. Check the correct size and sale configuration; do not assume that one passing size represents every size or variant.","Before bulk release, confirm the BOM, reference sample, test reports, labels, care instructions, and design revision. If the button, cord, coating, or trim supplier changes, reassess the risk and determine whether additional testing is required. These examples are training cases, not a complete legal checklist."],"deep":"A children's safety review should list details that can be pulled, detached, caught, or wrapped around the wearer. Review cords, buttons, zippers, decorations, and end pieces against the intended age group and target-market requirements. Assess trim attachment strength, accessibility, and risks after washing. Retain photographs, inspection results, and the decision for each identified issue.","labels":["Case Study: Reviewing Childrenswear Safety Before Release","Case Study: Reviewing Childrenswear Safety Before Release","Childrenswear detail-safety review","Hazard","Design review","Testing","Release decision","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Reviewing Childrenswear Safety Before Release\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Reviewing Childrenswear Safety Before Release</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Case Study: Reviewing Childrenswear Safety Before Release</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Childrenswear detail-safety review</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Hazard</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Design review</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Testing</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Release decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"49":{"title":"Case Study: Selecting Medical Apparel for the Intended Use Process","cat":"Advanced Practice","body":["Illustrative case: a healthcare facility orders gowns for use in patient-care areas. Before design begins, clarify whether the product is a uniform, procedure gown, or barrier garment; whether it is single-use or reusable; which fluids it may contact; and how it will be donned, removed, collected, laundered, or discarded.","Distinguish hygiene, laundering durability, and liquid or microbial barrier requirements. An ordinary lab coat or scrub garment does not automatically qualify as a protective gown. Barrier level must be defined by risk assessment and the relevant product standard, not inferred from the product name or colour.","Assess the complete construction: body fabric, seams, neckline, sleeves, cuffs, closures, intersections, and areas subject to tension. For reusable products, include the intended laundering and disinfection cycles in validation. For single-use products, control package integrity and storage conditions.","Build a test matrix for the intended purpose: shedding or particle testing where required, barrier testing where a claim is made, seam integrity, tensile strength, post-laundering durability, and dimensional and appearance checks. Limits and acceptance criteria must come from the relevant standard, purchasing specification, or competent authority.","Before release, confirm donning and doffing instructions, clean-to-soiled handling, labels, packaging, and lot traceability. Product testing does not replace the infection-control procedures of the user facility; operating instructions require approval by the healthcare organization."],"deep":"A medical-apparel case must begin with the use process: who wears the garment, in which area, what exposure occurs, how it is laundered or disinfected, and when it must be replaced. Select fabric for breathability, durability, lint generation, and cleanability. Define and separately verify any barrier claim, distinguishing the product from ordinary uniforms.","labels":["Case Study: Selecting Medical Apparel for the Intended Use Process","Case Study: Selecting Medical Apparel for the Intended Use Process","Medical-apparel use-process validation","Use process","Exposure assessment","Testing","Release decision","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Selecting Medical Apparel for the Intended Use Process\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Selecting Medical Apparel for the Intended Use Process</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Case Study: Selecting Medical Apparel for the Intended Use Process</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Medical-apparel use-process validation</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Use process</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Exposure assessment</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Testing</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Release decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"},"50":{"title":"Case Study: Soft Backpack Load-Path Analysis and Testing","cat":"Advanced Practice","body":["Illustrative case: a daily-use soft backpack has two shoulder straps, a carry handle, a laptop compartment, a reinforced base, and a main zipper. Rather than selecting only a high-strength fabric, the design team must define the expected load, carrying and lifting methods, placement, and possible uneven-load conditions.","Map the load path from the contents through the backpack body, base, strap attachments, seams, and reinforcement. Review the upper strap ends, lower strap anchors, carry handle, base corners, panel seams, zipper ends, and accessory attachment points. A strong shell fabric does not prove that the complete strap and seam assembly can carry the load.","Create a construction table listing each component, material, reinforcement layer, seam type, bartack or box-X construction, potential failure mode, and suitable test. Check thickness compatibility when sewing multiple layers, skipped-stitch risk, needle breakage, seam puckering, and stitching that cuts into webbing.","Design tests for the agreed use case: strap pull, static loading, repeated or dynamic loading, seam inspection, zipper operation, and abrasion. Record the load, fixture, duration or cycle count, failure location, and sample configuration. Do not use an arbitrary load as an acceptance limit; establish the test level from product requirements and the approved method.","During the pilot, monitor strap-position variation, reinforcement consistency, bartack quality, and operator handling. If webbing, thread, shell material, or sewing machine changes, reassess the load path and determine which tests must be repeated. The final record must link test samples to the BOM, revision, and material lots."],"deep":"For a soft backpack, map the load from the contents through the panels, base, straps, anchors, and body-contact areas. Define the service load and representative test method; assess strap pull-out, tearing at anchor points, base abrasion, and zipper durability. After load testing, inspect deformation and continued usability rather than recording only whether a component broke.","labels":["Case Study: Soft Backpack Load-Path Analysis and Testing","Case Study: Soft Backpack Load-Path Analysis and Testing","Backpack load path and validation","Load case","Load path","Testing","Inspection decision","Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use."],"diagram":"<figure class=\"phase17-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Soft Backpack Load-Path Analysis and Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Soft Backpack Load-Path Analysis and Testing</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#233e50\">Case Study: Soft Backpack Load-Path Analysis and Testing</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Backpack load path and validation</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Load case</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Load path</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Testing</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#edf3f6\" stroke=\"#54758a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#233e50\">Inspection decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Assess the specified configuration under its intended conditions of use.</text></svg></figure>"}}};

